/* Chapter 16 — canonical topics, questions, flashcards and exam prompts. */
IFL_DATA.registerChapter({"number":16,"title":"Takaful: An Alternative to Conventional Insurance","part":"part-iii","partRoman":"III","pages":[417,432],"minutes":55,"estimates":{"f1":55,"f2":45},"difficulty":"Intermediate","difficultyAlt":"intermediate","learningObjectives":[{"id":"lo-ch16-1","text":"Explain the need for risk cover in Islam and why conventional insurance is considered prohibited (Riba, Gharar, Qimar/Maisir).","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch16-2","text":"Explain the Shari’ah basis of Takaful: ‘Aqilah, Tabarru‘, Waqf and mutual help.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch16-3","text":"Describe how Takaful works for family and general Takaful.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch16-4","text":"Compare the Wakalah, Mudarabah, Wakalah–Mudarabah and Waqf/Wakalah–Waqf models and their Shari’ah issues.","origin":["f1","f2"],"altText":"Compare the Wakalah, Mudarabah, Waqf and Wakalah-Waqf models of Takaful and their respective Shari'ah issues"},{"id":"lo-ch16-5","text":"Compare Takaful with conventional insurance.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch16-6","text":"Assess the status, potential and challenges of the Takaful industry.","origin":["f1","f2"],"altText":"Assess the status, potential and key challenges facing the global Takaful industry"},{"id":"lo-ch16-7","text":"Explain why conventional insurance is considered non-compliant with Shari'ah","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch16-8","text":"Describe the Shari'ah basis of Takaful, including Tabarru' and Waqf","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch16-9","text":"Explain how the Takaful system works, including the treatment of underwriting surplus/deficit","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch16-10","text":"Compare Takaful and conventional insurance across objectives, structure and investment policy","origin":["f2"]}],"why":"Takaful completes the Islamic finance cycle — Ijarah assets, housing finance and trade all need cover. Exams ask why insurance is prohibited, how Takaful differs, and which operating model is closest to Shari’ah and why.","whyExpanded":"Takaful completes the cycle of Islamic finance by providing a Shari'ah-compliant alternative to conventional insurance, which Islamic banks' clients and asset-backed contracts (e.g. auto Ijarah, housing finance) routinely require. Understanding why conventional insurance is prohibited, and how mutual risk-sharing through Tabarru' and Waqf offers an alternative, is essential for anyone assessing Islamic financial institutions' risk management and product completeness.","overview":"The chapter argues that seeking protection against risk is consistent with Islam, identifies Riba, Gharar and gambling as the reasons conventional insurance is prohibited, and grounds Takaful in ‘Aqilah, Tabarru‘ and Waqf. It explains how Takaful funds work, evaluates operating models (the author's preferred convergence is the Wakalah–Waqf model), compares Takaful with insurance, and reviews the industry's status, potential and challenges, with an appendix of Fatawa.","summary":"Seeking protection against misfortune is consistent with Islam — the Prophet told the Bedouin to tie his camel first — but conventional insurance is held unlawful by the overwhelming majority of scholars because of Riba, Gharar/Khatar, Qimar and Maisir and the invalid transfer of risk in a commutative contract. Takaful replaces it with cooperative risk-sharing grounded in ‘Aqilah, Tabarru‘ and Waqf: participants donate to a fund that compensates defined losses; the underwriting surplus or deficit belongs to participants; the operator earns a Wakalah fee and/or a Mudarib share of investment profit and provides Qard al Hasan for deficits. Family Takaful splits contributions into protection and investment parts; general Takaful treats the whole contribution as a donation. The Mudarabah model and surplus-sharing variants are criticised for making the operator a risk-taker; research led by Mufti Taqi Usmani favours the Waqf or Wakalah–Waqf model. The industry has grown fast since 1979 but lags banking; its challenges are awareness, capital, people, re-Takaful, investments, standardisation, regulation and Shari’ah governance. (Chapter synthesis — the chapter has no separate summary section.)","summaryExpanded":"Chapter 16 explains why conventional insurance is Shari'ah-non-compliant (Riba, Qimar/Maisir and Gharar in a commutative contract) and how Takaful offers a mutual-help alternative built on Tabarru' (donation) and Waqf (endowment), with historical roots in the Arab institution of 'Aqilah. It walks through how the Takaful system operates - contribution, fund pooling, claims payment and surplus distribution - and evaluates the Wakalah, Mudarabah, Waqf and Wakalah-Waqf operating models, concluding that the combined Wakalah-Waqf model is closest to Shari'ah principles because it avoids the underwriting-surplus-without-loss problem that troubles the pure Wakalah and Mudarabah models. The chapter closes by comparing Takaful to conventional insurance and surveying the industry's real status, potential and unresolved challenges - awareness, capitalization, re-Takaful scarcity, human resources and standardization.","summarySection":null,"takeaways":["Precaution is consistent with Tawakkul: ‘Tie your camel’.","Insurance is prohibited for Riba, Gharar, Qimar/Maisir.","Takaful = Tabarru‘ + mutual help; UWS/UWL to participants.","Waqf lets donors benefit — preferred basis.","Mudarabah model: operator takes surplus without bearing deficit — objectionable.","Box 16.1: 25–30% fee, 70–75% invested, 40:60 Mudarabah split (example).","Family Takaful benefits go to heirs by inheritance rules.","Biggest challenge: awareness.","Conventional insurance is considered non-compliant mainly due to Riba, Qimar/Maisir and Gharar within a commutative contract","Takaful replaces the commutative insurance contract with Tabarru' (donation) and Waqf (endowment), rooted in the historical 'Aqilah institution","The settled scholarly consensus is that the whole underwriting surplus/deficit belongs to participants, not the operator","Pure Wakalah and pure Mudarabah Takaful models both face documented Shari'ah objections around the operator sharing surplus without bearing loss","The Wakalah-Waqf combined model, endorsed by research under Shaikh Muhammad Taqi Usmani, is considered nearest to Shari'ah principles","Takaful has a built-in anti-overpricing mechanism since surplus always returns to participants in proportion to their contributions","Takaful has grown faster than the global insurance industry (10-20% vs 5% p.a.) but still lags Islamic banking's global success","Major remaining challenges include public awareness, capitalization for family Takaful, re-Takaful scarcity, trained human resources and cross-jurisdiction standardization"],"takeawayOrigins":["f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2"],"checklist":["Can you rebut the ‘insurance contradicts Tawakkul’ argument?","Can you explain Riba, Gharar and Maisir in insurance?","Can you explain ‘Aqilah, Tabarru‘ and Waqf as bases?","Can you describe family vs general Takaful contributions?","Can you compare the four Takaful models?","Can you explain Box 16.1?","Can you tabulate Takaful vs insurance?","Can you list Takaful challenges?"],"topics":[{"id":"t16.1","section":"16.1","title":"Introduction","pages":[417,417],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["takaful"],"intuition":"Why did Islamic banks need Takaful?","simpleExplanation":"Insurance mitigates risks for businesses and individuals, and financial institutions need cover. When Islamic banking began in the 1970s, it needed an alternative to conventional insurance, which involves Riba, Gharar and gambling. Takaful was developed to fill this gap. The chapter introduces main features rather than technical details.","academicExplanation":["Bankers are mostly not involved in Takaful business; the chapter aims to introduce the Shari’ah-compliant system.","Insurance has become a need for businesses, individuals and financial institutions that want to reduce the impact of losses and catastrophes. When Islamic banking began in the 1970s, it needed a Shari’ah-compliant alternative, because conventional insurance was seen to involve Riba, Gharar and gambling. Takaful was developed to fill this gap, and many Takaful companies now operate worldwide (p. 417). The author says the chapter only introduces the main features of Takaful for bankers, who are usually not involved in Takaful business. It covers why insurance is objected to, the need for and evolution of Takaful, its Shari’ah basis, models, status and challenges. It does not cover the technical side of Takaful operations (p. 417)."],"examExplanation":"Takaful developed as a Shari’ah-compliant alternative to insurance (Riba, Gharar, gambling) to complete Islamic finance.","keyPoints":["Insurance = life and general (marine, fire, accident).","Conventional insurance has two broad categories: life (whole-life and endowment policies) and general (marine, fire, accident) (p. 417 n. 1)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Why a bank needs Takaful","kind":"practice","text":"An Islamic bank leasing 2,000 cars must insure them under law. Conventional insurance involves Riba, Gharar and Maisir, so it needs a Shari’ah-compliant alternative — Takaful (p. 417)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t16.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which elements make conventional insurance objectionable, per Chapter 16?","options":["Riba, Gharar and gambling","Only taxes","Only high premiums","Agents' commissions"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"p. 417."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch16-t1"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch16-t1","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"16.1-16.2","sectionTitle":"Introduction; The Need for Takaful Cover","title":"The Need for Takaful Cover","covers":["t16.1","t16.2","t16.2.1"],"overview":"Insurance is a genuine human need to mitigate risk, and Muslim avoidance of conventional insurance stems from two reasons - one a myth, one a genuine Shari'ah concern - creating the need for a Shari'ah-compliant alternative.","simpleExplanation":"People naturally want protection against life's risks, and Islam doesn't reject that instinct - the Prophet himself told a man to tie up his camel before trusting in God. Muslims avoided conventional insurance partly because of a mistaken belief that any insurance questions God's will (a myth) and partly because conventional insurance genuinely involves Riba, Gharar and gambling (a real problem) - so scholars developed Takaful to meet the real need without the real problem.","academicExplanation":"Insurance became a necessary part of business and the financial system as society developed; Islamic banking's emergence in the 1970s created a parallel need for a Shari'ah-compliant insurance alternative, given conventional insurance's association with Riba, Gharar and gambling. Muslim societies historically avoided commercial insurance for two reasons: (1) a belief that mutual help among community members and trust in Allah's will/destiny make insurance unnecessary - the author labels this 'only a myth', since Shari'ah in fact encourages finding ways to avoid catastrophe (citing Qur'an 2:185, 4:28, and the Hadith of the Prophet telling a man to 'tie your camel first, then put your trust in Allah'); (2) the genuine prohibition of Riba, Gharar and gambling, plus conventional insurers' involvement in other Haram businesses (alcohol, pork, indecent entertainment) - this reason is 'genuine and must be observed'. Because avoiding cover altogether is not realistic in modern life, scholars developed a scheme to enable risk protection without violating Shari'ah. The insurance business is mostly conducted by non-bank financial institutions (NBFIs), with commercial banks in most countries not permitted to conduct insurance business directly, though all banks/NBFIs and businesses/individuals increasingly need insurance services. Islamic banking emerged in the 1960s-70s, but Islamic insurance (Takaful) began no earlier than 1979 - reflecting demand arising because banking and insurance complement each other operationally, as well as positive implications for individuals/households who had avoided conventional insurance on belief grounds.","examExplanation":"Two reasons Muslims avoided conventional insurance: (1) myth - belief that trusting God/community mutual-help makes insurance unnecessary (rejected by the Hadith of tying the camel); (2) genuine - Riba, Gharar, gambling and Haram-business involvement in conventional insurers. Takaful emerged ~1979 (later than Islamic banking, 1960s-70s) to fill this gap.","keyPoints":["Insurance has become a necessary part of business and the financial system as society developed","Reason 1 for avoiding conventional insurance ('it's unnecessary since we should trust God') is described by the author as 'only a myth'","Reason 2 (Riba, Gharar, gambling and involvement in Haram businesses) is described as 'genuine and must be observed'","The Prophet's advice to 'tie your camel first, then put your trust in Allah' is cited to show Islam does not oppose taking precautions against risk","Takaful began no earlier than 1979, roughly a decade after Islamic banking's 1960s-70s emergence","Insurance business is mostly conducted by NBFIs, with commercial banks in most countries not permitted to conduct insurance business directly"],"definitions":[{"term":"Underwriting Surplus (UWS)","definition":"The net amount of money the insurer/Takaful fund retains from premiums/contributions paid by policyholders after payment of claims, if any."},{"term":"Underwriting Loss (UWL)","definition":"The amount of money the insurer/Takaful fund has to pay in claims in excess of the premiums/contributions received from policyholders."}],"conditions":[],"principles":["Shari'ah intends to save human beings from hardship and accepts the basic safety requirement of protecting self, offspring, wealth and against disease, illiteracy and poverty"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"The Prophet's Camel Advice","kind":"textbook","text":"When the Prophet (pbuh) saw a man leaving his camel untied in the jungle and asked why, the man replied 'I put my trust in Allah.' The Prophet responded: 'Tie your camel first, then put your trust in Allah' - illustrating that taking precautions against risk is not against Islamic belief."}],"commonConfusions":["Believing that trust in Allah/destiny means seeking risk protection is un-Islamic - the chapter explicitly calls this a myth"],"distinctions":["The myth-based objection to insurance (belief/trust argument) versus the genuine Shari'ah objection (Riba/Gharar/gambling) - only the second is a valid basis for rejecting conventional insurance"],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Gharar","Maisir","Qimar","Takaful"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.1-16.2","pages":[417,418]}}]},{"id":"t16.2","section":"16.2","title":"The Need for Takaful Cover","pages":[417,418],"tier":"core","concepts":["takaful","tawakkul"],"intuition":"Does buying cover contradict trust in Allah?","simpleExplanation":"Muslim societies avoided commercial insurance for two reasons: a belief that mutual help and trust in destiny make it unnecessary, and the prohibitions of Riba, Gharar and gambling (plus insurers' investment in forbidden businesses). The author regards the second as genuine and the first as a myth: Islam requires reasonable precautions — ‘Tie your camel first, then put your trust in Allah’ — and Shari’ah intends ease (Qur’an 2:185; 4:28). Islamic insurance began only in 1979, largely in response to IFIs' needs.","academicExplanation":["Insurance is mostly conducted by NBFIs; banks are generally not allowed to do insurance but need it as a regulatory and business requirement. Takaful also benefits individuals who avoided insurance on religious grounds. The rule of necessity supports developing a compliant scheme.","Muslim societies have tended to avoid commercial insurance for two reasons (pp. 417–418). The first is a belief that it is unnecessary, since Muslims should help victims of misfortune, and that true faith in Allah and destiny makes cover pointless. The second is that the prohibitions of Riba, Gharar and gambling appear to rule it out, and insurers also invest in forbidden businesses. The author accepts the second reason as genuine but calls the first a myth. Islam requires people to avoid catastrophe where possible and to lighten the burden on their families. The Qur’an says Allah intends ease, not hardship (2:185), and wishes to lighten the burden (4:28). The Prophet (pbuh) told a man to tie his camel first and then trust in Allah (p. 418). Islamic insurance began only in 1979, well after Islamic banking. This suggests Takaful arose mainly because Islamic banks needed risk cover, since banking and insurance complement each other (p. 418)."],"examExplanation":"Two reasons for avoiding insurance — belief (myth, per author) and prohibitions (genuine). Hadith: tie your camel. Qur’an 2:185, 4:28. Takaful from 1979.","keyPoints":["Author's view: seeking cover is not against Tawakkul.","Commercial banks are generally not allowed to do insurance business, but all financial institutions need insurance, by regulation or business necessity (p. 418)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Tie your camel","kind":"textbook","text":"A man left his camel untied, saying he trusted in Allah. The Prophet (pbuh) told him, “Tie your camel first, then put your trust in Allah” — precaution is part of trust, not against it (p. 418)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Seeking cover against loss contradicts trust in Allah.","right":"The author calls this a myth. Precaution is required (“Tie your camel first, then put your trust in Allah”), and the Shari’ah aims to remove hardship (p. 418)."}],"relatedTopics":["t16.2.1","t3.3.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which hadith does the author cite to show that precaution is consistent with Tawakkul?","options":["‘Leave your offspring wealthy’","‘Tie your camel first, then put your trust in Allah’","‘Muslims are bound by their conditions’","‘Earnings are concomitant with risk’"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 418."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"debate":[{"issue":"Is seeking insurance cover contrary to trust in Allah and destiny?","criticism":"Many believe true faith and mutual help make cover unnecessary and that insuring questions Allah's decree.","response":"The author calls this a myth: Islam directs believers to avoid catastrophe where possible and to lighten burdens; the Prophet said ‘Tie your camel first, then put your trust in Allah’; Shari’ah intends ease.","alternative":"Seek cover through a scheme free of Riba, Gharar and gambling — Takaful.","takeaway":"The objection lies in insurance's contract elements, not in seeking protection itself."}]},{"id":"t16.2.1","section":"16.2.1","title":"Why Conventional Insurance is Prohibited","pages":[418,420],"tier":"core","concepts":["riba","gharar","maisir"],"intuition":"Where exactly do Riba, Gharar and gambling appear in a premium-for-claim contract?","simpleExplanation":"An overwhelming majority of scholars hold commercial insurance unlawful because of Riba (directly — premium vs sum insured; indirectly — investment in interest), Qimar and Maisir (one side gains at the other's loss; gains from chance), Gharar/Khatar (contingent rights and liabilities in a commutative contract) and invalid transfer of risk from insured to insurer. The insurer owns an underwriting surplus or bears an underwriting loss — a money stake. Premiums are lost if no claim occurs or if a life policy is cancelled early.","academicExplanation":["History: marine insurance began around the end of the 12th century and became formal in the 17th; Ibn Abdin (19th century) first discussed it in detail and did not approve it.","Differences of opinion arose because early jurists were not given full technical background and there is no direct textual reference; as knowledge grew, the majority concluded it is unlawful.","Khatar: stipulating transfer of property or profit on an uncertain event where commercial benefit is on both sides — liabilities contingent, delivery not controlled, payment uncertain. Qimar: one party's profit depends on the other's loss. Maisir: gains from chance and speculation rather than work or responsibility.","Gharar is prohibited in commutative contracts, and insurance is commutative, so uncertainty invalidates it. Legal requirements (e.g. insured Ijarah assets), client demands and housing finance needs made an alternative urgent."],"examExplanation":"Prohibited because of Riba (direct/indirect), Gharar/Khatar, Qimar/Maisir, invalid risk transfer; commutative contract; UWS/UWL owned by insurer. Ibn Abdin first jurist to discuss insurance.","keyPoints":["Gharar vitiates commutative contracts; Takaful converts the contract to donation-based cooperation.","Ibn Abdin (19th century) was the first jurist to write in detail on commercial (marine) insurance, and he did not approve it (pp. 418–419).","Scholars differed because early muftis lacked details of insurance and there is no direct text; with greater knowledge the overwhelming majority judged it unlawful (p. 419).","Marine insurance probably began at the end of the 12th century and became formalised in the 17th (p. 418)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Underwriting surplus (UWS)","definition":"Premiums remaining after payment of claims."},{"term":"Underwriting loss (UWL)","definition":"Claims paid in excess of premiums received."},{"term":"Qimar","definition":"A deal in which one party's profit depends on the other's loss."}],"examples":[{"title":"Unclaimed general policy","kind":"textbook","text":"If no claim is made on a non-life policy, the insurer keeps almost all the premium. The policyholder’s right and liability were contingent throughout, which is Khatar (pp. 419–420)."},{"title":"Early cancellation of life policy","kind":"textbook","text":"A life policyholder who cancels in the first two or three years usually loses all premiums paid. If the insurer cancels, it gives only a proportional refund (p. 419)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Gharar invalidates any contract involving uncertainty, including gifts.","right":"Gharar is prohibited in commercial or commutative contracts. Conventional insurance is commutative, so its uncertainty invalidates it (p. 420)."}],"relatedTopics":["t3.2","t4.2.3","t16.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Indirect Riba in conventional insurance arises from:","options":["Paying claims","Investment of funds in interest-based businesses","Agents' fees","Policy documents"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 419."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch16-t2"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch16-t2","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"16.2.1","sectionTitle":"Why Conventional Insurance is Prohibited","title":"Why Conventional Insurance is Prohibited","covers":["t16.2.1"],"overview":"An overwhelming majority of Shari'ah scholars find conventional insurance unlawful due to Riba, Qimar/Maisir, Gharar and invalid risk transfer, making it a commutative contract vitiated by uncertainty.","simpleExplanation":"Conventional insurance is rejected mainly because of three intertwined problems: interest hidden in how premiums and payouts work, gambling-like uncertainty about who gains and who loses, and genuine uncertainty about what each side is really giving and getting - all of which make it a kind of exploitative money-stake rather than a fair mutual protection scheme.","academicExplanation":"Marine insurance, the first commercial insurance form, began around the late 12th century, formalizing in the 17th century. Among Islamic jurists, Ibn Abidin (19th century) was the first to write about modern commercial insurance in detail, disapproving it from the Shari'ah viewpoint. Differing scholarly views arose partly because early jurists lacked full knowledge of insurance's structure/complexity, and partly because there is no direct Qur'anic/Sunnah reference; as Islamic economics/finance matured, an overwhelming majority of scholars concluded commercial insurance is unlawful due to: Riba (interest) - both directly (an excess on one side between premiums and sum insured; loss of premiums if a policyholder cancels non-life or life cover, versus only proportional refund if the insurer terminates) and indirectly (insurer investment in interest-based businesses); Qimar/Maisir (gambling) - monetary gain from mere chance/speculation, not work or real business; Gharar (excessive uncertainty, synonymous with Khatar) - uncertainty about the subject matter, price, and rights/liabilities of parties, found where liability is uncertain/contingent, delivery is outside a party's control, or payment from one side is uncertain; and invalid transfer of risk from insured to insurer. A conventional insurance policyholder faces Khatar because both liability and right remain contingent - premiums are lost entirely if the insured event does not occur (general insurance), and in life policies a policyholder generally loses all premiums paid if cancelling within the first two or three years. The insurer itself does not know in advance how much it will owe (and often the insured doesn't know how much they will ultimately pay), and the insurer realizes an underwriting surplus (UWS) or bears an underwriting loss (UWL) depending on whether claims are lower or higher than premiums received - a monetary/commercial outcome where any party gains at the other's cost, motivated by 'chance profit', which is what makes the contract a money stake or gamble. Since conventional insurance is a commutative (exchange) contract, any involvement of Gharar invalidates it (unlike a purely charitable/donative contract, where uncertainty is more tolerated). Despite these prohibitions, modern life requires risk cover (e.g. Islamic banking contracts often legally require insurance of underlying assets - auto Ijarah, storage, shipment); Islamic banks' clients also wanted to avoid conventional insurance's interest exposure, and there was a need for a life-insurance alternative (e.g. for housing finance) - making Takaful's development necessary to complete the cycle of Islamic finance.","examExplanation":"Four prohibitions in conventional insurance: (1) Riba - direct (premium/payout mismatch) and indirect (interest-based investment of premiums); (2) Qimar/Maisir - gain from chance, not work; (3) Gharar/Khatar - uncertain liability, delivery or payment; (4) invalid risk transfer. Because insurance is a commutative contract, Gharar within it is fatal (unlike in a purely charitable contract).","keyPoints":["An overwhelming majority of Shari'ah scholars consider commercial insurance unlawful due to Riba, Qimar/Maisir, Gharar and invalid risk transfer","Riba operates directly (mismatch between premiums and sum insured/lost premiums on cancellation) and indirectly (insurer investing premiums in interest-based businesses)","Gharar/Khatar exists where liability is uncertain or contingent, delivery is outside a party's control, or payment from one side is uncertain","Because conventional insurance is a commutative (exchange) contract, the presence of Gharar invalidates it","The insurer's realization of underwriting surplus (UWS) or bearing of underwriting loss (UWL), driven by 'chance profit', is what makes conventional insurance resemble a gamble","Practical necessity (e.g. legally required insurance for auto Ijarah, storage, shipment) made a Shari'ah-compliant alternative to insurance urgently necessary"],"definitions":[{"term":"Khatar","definition":"Uncertainty involving the stipulated transfer of ownership of property or profitability in a deal where commercial benefit is involved on both sides for an uncertain event; found where liability is uncertain/contingent, delivery is outside a party's control, or payment is uncertain."},{"term":"Qimar","definition":"A situation found in a deal where the profit of one party is dependent on the loss of the other, involving Maisir (gain from mere chance, speculation or conjecture rather than work or real business)."}],"conditions":[],"principles":["Gharar/uncertainty is prohibited specifically when involved in commercial/commutative contracts, which conventional insurance is"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Assuming Gharar is prohibited in all contracts equally - the chapter notes it specifically invalidates commutative (exchange) contracts like conventional insurance, distinct from donative/charitable arrangements"],"distinctions":["Direct Riba (premium/payout structure) versus indirect Riba (insurer's interest-based investment of collected premiums)"],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Qimar","Maisir","Gharar","Takaful","Auto Ijarah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","pages":[418,419,420]}}],"table":{"caption":"Prohibited elements in conventional insurance (pp. 419–420)","head":["Element","How it arises"],"rows":[["Riba (direct)","Excess in the exchange of premiums for the sum insured"],["Riba (indirect)","Insurer invests premiums in interest-based business"],["Gharar / Khatar","Liabilities and rights contingent; insurer does not know what it will owe"],["Qimar / Maisir","One party’s gain depends on the other’s loss; chance profit"],["Invalid risk transfer","Risk sold from insured to insurer in a commutative contract"]]}},{"id":"t16.3","section":"16.3","title":"The Shari’ah Basis of Takaful","pages":[420,421],"tier":"core","concepts":["takaful","tabarru","waqf","aqilah"],"intuition":"How can a ‘premium’ become a gift?","simpleExplanation":"Ta’mein (reassurance, guarantee) gave way to Takaful — a contributory arrangement where members' losses are covered from a pool through mutual help. Shaikh Abu Zahra held cooperative and social insurance legitimate and non-cooperative insurance unacceptable; the OIC Fiqh Council approved cooperative Takaful in 1985. Foundations: ‘Aqilah (kinsmen sharing blood money), Qasamah and Mawalat; early Muslim traders' mutual funds for voyage losses. Tabarru‘ (donation) removes Gharar; Waqf (endowment) lets donors also benefit. Surplus/deficit belongs to members; the operator earns a fee and/or share of investment profit.","academicExplanation":["Participants relinquish, as Tabarru‘, all or part of their contributions to help fellow participants who suffer a defined loss.","Waqf: retention of property for charitable or specified beneficiaries; a separate entity; cannot be sold; usufruct assigned; a donor can benefit. In Takaful, beneficiaries are the Waqf creator and contributing members.","Losses of the unfortunate few are shared by contributions of the fortunate many exposed to the same risk; the manager/trustee pays claims and invests compliantly."],"examExplanation":"Takaful basis: mutual help, ‘Aqilah, Tabarru‘, Waqf; Abu Zahra; OIC Fiqh Council 1985. UWS/UWL to members; operator fee/investment profit share.","keyPoints":["Investment profit ≠ underwriting surplus.","Shaikh Abu Zahra held cooperative and social insurance legitimate, and non-cooperative insurance invalid because of gambling, temptation and usury (p. 420).","The OIC Islamic Fiqh Council approved cooperative Takaful in 1985 (p. 420).","There are three kinds of Waqf: religious, philanthropic and family. A Waqf is a separate entity; its property cannot be sold, and only the usufruct is assigned (p. 421).","The UWS/UWL belongs to the group. The manager earns a fee and/or a share of investment profit, which is distinct from the underwriting result (p. 421)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Takaful","definition":"Cooperative risk-sharing in which participants donate (Tabarru‘) contributions to a pool that compensates members for defined losses."},{"term":"Tabarru‘","definition":"Donation; the contribution a participant relinquishes to help fellow participants."},{"term":"‘Aqilah","definition":"Kinsmen's shared responsibility for blood money — a precedent for Takaful approved by the Prophet (pbuh)."},{"term":"Waqf","definition":"Endowment: property retained for beneficiaries, a separate entity whose corpus cannot be sold; used as the basis of a Takaful fund."},{"term":"Ta’mein","definition":"The approximate Arabic word for insurance: to reassure, safeguard and guarantee through indemnity. It implies guarantee more than cooperative loss-sharing."},{"term":"Qasamah","definition":"An oath taken from the kinsmen of a murdered person; cited as an early practice of shared responsibility."},{"term":"Mawalat","definition":"A contract in which one party bequeaths property to another, who in turn pays any blood money the first may owe."}],"examples":[{"title":"Traders’ mutual fund","kind":"textbook","text":"In the early second century AH, Arab traders sailing to Asia contributed to a fund to help any member who suffered mishaps or robbery at sea (p. 421)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A Waqf donor can never benefit from the Waqf.","right":"Under Waqf principles a donor may be among the beneficiaries. In Takaful the beneficiaries are the Waqf’s creator and the contributing group (p. 421)."}],"relatedTopics":["t16.3.1","t16.4.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In which year did the OIC Islamic Fiqh Council approve cooperative Takaful?","options":["1979","1985","1995","2000"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 420."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch16-t3"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch16-t3","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"16.3","sectionTitle":"The Shari'ah Basis of Takaful","title":"The Shari'ah Basis of Takaful","covers":["t16.3","t16.3.1"],"overview":"Takaful is built on Tabarru' (donation) and Waqf (endowment), drawing on the historical Arab institution of 'Aqilah (shared kinship responsibility), with mutual help - not profit - as its central objective.","simpleExplanation":"Takaful reworks insurance into a mutual-help arrangement: instead of paying a premium to a company that promises to pay you back, participants donate part of their contribution to a shared pool, drawing on old Arab customs of tribes pooling resources to help members in trouble, and the goal is mutual assistance, not making a profit from someone else's misfortune.","academicExplanation":"'Ta'mein', the approximate Arabic equivalent of insurance, denotes reassurance/guarantee through indemnity, closer to a guarantee than cooperative loss-sharing; the concept that eventually gained wide scholarly acceptance is Takaful, requiring the main insurance contract to be converted into a contributory arrangement where member losses are covered from a Takaful pool on the basis of mutual help and sacrifice. Shaikh Abu Zahra (20th century jurist) concluded a cooperative/social insurance scheme is legitimate in principle, while noncooperative insurance is unacceptable for containing gambling, temptation and usury traits that invalidate the contract; the Islamic Fiqh Council of the OIC approved the Takaful system based on mutual cooperation as an insurance alternative in 1985. Takaful draws on historical Arab institutions: 'Aqilah (kinsmen sharing financial responsibility, e.g. for blood money - the Prophet himself paid blood money of 100 camels of Sadaqah in one Qasamah case), and Muwalat (a contract where one party agreed to bequeath property to another in exchange for the other paying any eventual blood money due). Contemporary jurists see 'Aqilah's principle of shared responsibility as the foundation of Takaful, practised and approved by the Prophet; there was also an early second-century-Hijri institution of Arab traders mutually contributing to a fund to help group members facing sea-voyage mishaps or robberies. On these bases, Takaful embodies shared responsibility, common benefit and mutual solidarity, with each policyholder paying a subscription to assist fellow members in need. Since Islamic finance theory rejects Gharar/uncertainty in commutative contracts, the concept of Tabarru' (donation) is incorporated as the arrangement's main ingredient: a Takaful participant agrees to relinquish, as Tabarru', the whole or a proportion of their contribution, fulfilling their obligation of mutual help toward fellow participants who suffer a defined loss. A second supporting institution is Waqf (endowment) - the retention of property for a charitable/humanitarian objective or a specified group (religious, philanthropic or family Waqf); Waqf becomes a separate entity able to accept/transfer ownership, with ownership permanently transferred from the creator (property cannot be sold, only its usufruct assigned to beneficiaries), though a member/donor can also benefit as one of the beneficiaries. In Takaful arrangements, the Waqf's beneficiaries are the Waqf's creator and the contributing group members seeking mutual help. Losses of the unfortunate few are shared by contributions of the fortunate many exposed to the same risk on a cooperative risk-sharing basis; funds are used by the manager/trustee for claims and Shari'ah-compliant business, with the underwriting surplus/deficit belonging to the group members, and the pool manager earning a fee and/or a share of 'investment profit' (distinct from UWS/UWL). Main objective (16.3.1): the policyholders' primary objective is mutual help, not profit or windfall gains, unlike conventional insurance; participants in family or general Takaful agree to help one another from their contributions when any member faces catastrophe or a defined loss, while operators (as a business venture) may earn fees/profit shares for their services. Family Takaful is consistent with the Prophet's teaching 'It is better for you to leave your offspring wealthy than to leave them poor, asking others for help', and his encouragement of providing for widows, orphans and the poor. There is no Shari'ah issue in viewing Takaful as a business when conducted with compliance, transparency and fairness to all stakeholders.","examExplanation":"Ta'mein (guarantee-like) vs Takaful (mutual cooperative sharing) - Takaful is the concept scholars accepted. Historical roots: 'Aqilah (kinship shared responsibility for blood money) and early Arab trader mutual-help funds. Core mechanisms: Tabarru' (donation, avoids Gharar) + Waqf (endowment holding the fund, usufruct assigned to beneficiaries). Main objective: mutual help, not profit, for participants; operators may earn fees/profit share as a business.","keyPoints":["Ta'mein (guarantee-like) was considered but Takaful (mutual cooperative sharing) is the concept that gained wide Shari'ah scholarly acceptance","The OIC's Islamic Fiqh Council approved the Takaful system based on mutual cooperation as an insurance alternative in 1985","'Aqilah (kinship shared responsibility, e.g. for blood money) is seen by contemporary jurists as the historical foundation of Takaful","Tabarru' (donation) is the main ingredient enabling Takaful to avoid the Gharar problem that invalidates conventional insurance","Waqf (endowment) holds the Takaful fund as a separate entity; its beneficiaries are the Waqf's creator and the contributing group members","The main objective of Takaful for policyholders is mutual help, not profit or windfall gains - though operators may legitimately earn fees/profit shares as a business"],"definitions":[{"term":"Tabarru'","definition":"Donation; in Takaful, the whole or a proportion of a participant's contribution which they agree to relinquish, enabling the arrangement to avoid the Gharar (uncertainty) that would otherwise invalidate a commutative insurance contract."},{"term":"Waqf (in Takaful)","definition":"An endowment - the retention of property for a charitable/humanitarian objective or specified group - used in Takaful to hold the fund as a separate legal entity whose beneficiaries are the Waqf's creator and the contributing participant group."},{"term":"'Aqilah","definition":"A historical Arab institution of kinsmen sharing financial responsibility (e.g. for blood money), approved by the Prophet and seen by contemporary jurists as the foundational principle underlying Takaful."}],"conditions":[],"principles":["Losses of the unfortunate few are shared by the contributions of the fortunate many exposed to the same risk, on a cooperative risk-sharing basis","The main objective of Takaful, from the policyholders' perspective, is mutual help rather than profit or windfall gain"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"The Prophet's Blood Money Payment (Qasamah)","kind":"textbook","text":"In one Qasamah case, the Prophet (pbuh) paid blood money of one hundred camels of Sadaqah, illustrating the historical 'Aqilah principle of shared kinship responsibility that jurists see as foundational to Takaful."}],"commonConfusions":["Treating Takaful as simply 'insurance with a different name' rather than recognizing its structurally different foundation in donation (Tabarru') and mutual help rather than a commutative exchange contract"],"distinctions":["Ta'mein (a guarantee-like concept, closer to conventional insurance) versus Takaful (mutual cooperative sharing) - only the latter gained broad Shari'ah acceptance"],"relatedConcepts":["Tabarru'","Waqf","'Aqilah","Mudarabah","Wakalah","Gharar"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420,421,422]}}]},{"id":"t16.3.1","section":"16.3.1","title":"Main Objective of the Takaful System","pages":[422,422],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["takaful"],"intuition":"Is family Takaful the same as ‘insuring your life’?","simpleExplanation":"From the participants' view the objective is mutual help, not profit or windfall gains. Operators may earn fees or profit shares as a business, and participants share realised profit after covering members' losses. Family Takaful is not against piety — it provides for dependants, in line with the Hadith ‘It is better to leave your offspring wealthy than poor’ and the Prophet's praise of those who look after widows and the poor.","academicExplanation":["Common pools of Muslim traders to compensate robbery or misfortune on journeys illustrate mutual protection; Takaful may be conducted as a business with compliance, transparency and fairness.","For policyholders, the aim of Takaful is mutual help, not profit or windfall gain. In family and general Takaful alike, participants agree to help one another from their contributions when a member suffers a defined loss (p. 422). Takaful may still be run as a business. Operators earn fees and/or profit shares, and participants may share realised profit after members’ losses are met. This raises no Shari’ah issue if it is done with compliance, transparency and fairness (p. 422). A family Takaful policy does not “insure one’s life”. It provides for one’s dependants, in line with the Hadith that it is better to leave heirs wealthy than dependent on others. The Prophet (pbuh) also praised those who look after widows and the poor (p. 422)."],"examExplanation":"Objective: mutual help (Tabarru‘) not profit; family Takaful supports dependants (Hadith on leaving offspring wealthy).","keyPoints":["Business operation is acceptable if compliant and fair."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Providing for dependants","kind":"textbook","text":"A father takes family Takaful so that his children are cared for if he dies, in line with the Hadith that it is better to leave heirs wealthy than dependent on others (p. 422)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Taking a family Takaful policy is a lack of piety because it insures one’s life.","right":"It is a means of safeguarding offspring, consistent with the Prophet’s saying about leaving heirs wealthy (p. 422)."}],"relatedTopics":["t16.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"The main objective of Takaful from participants' view is:","options":["Windfall gains","Mutual help","Tax savings","Speculation"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 422."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t16.4","section":"16.4","title":"How the Takaful System Works","pages":[422,423],"tier":"core","concepts":["takaful","family-takaful","retakaful"],"intuition":"Where does each rupee of a family Takaful contribution go?","simpleExplanation":"The operator acts as Wakil or Mudarib. Claims are paid from the Takaful fund; the underwriting surplus/deficit belongs to participants. Family Takaful contributions are split into a protection part (donation to the fund/Waqf) and an investment part (Mudarabah, individually owned, returned with profit); under Wakalah there is also a management-fee part. In general Takaful the whole contribution is a donation. The company gives Qard al Hasan if claims exceed fund and reserves. Re-Takaful follows the same bases.","academicExplanation":["Is Tabarru‘ the same as Sadaqah? Not necessarily: Mudarabah-model operators see the fund as a separate entity where the protection part is conditional on claims and surplus may be returned; in the Waqf model, donors relinquish ownership but, unlike Sadaqah, can benefit as beneficiaries — hence the Waqf model is preferred.","Early operators did not distinguish UWS from investment profit; scholars now hold the entire UWS/UWL belongs to participants, with the operator taking a fee and/or share of investment profit.","A Takaful company acts as trustee or manager on a Wakalah or Mudarabah basis. Participants contribute to the Takaful fund, claims are paid from it, and the underwriting surplus or deficit belongs to the participants. How investment profit is distributed depends on the Wakalah or Mudarabah basis (p. 422). In family Takaful under Mudarabah, contributions are split into a protection part and a savings/investment part. Under Wakalah there is a third part, the management fee (p. 422). The protection part works on donation, so individual rights give way to the Waqf. In the investment part individual rights remain, and the balance plus net profit is paid at maturity or earlier. In general Takaful the whole contribution is a donation for protection. If claims exceed the fund and reserves, the company lends Qard al Hasan (pp. 422–423). Re-Takaful follows the same Tabarru‘, Waqf and Mudarabah bases (p. 423)."],"examExplanation":"Family Takaful: protection (donation) + investment (Mudarabah) [+ fee under Wakalah]. General: whole contribution donation. Qard al Hasan for deficits. UWS/UWL to participants; operator: fee/investment profit share.","keyPoints":["Waqf model preferred because donors can still benefit."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Splitting a family Takaful contribution","kind":"textbook","text":"Under a Wakalah basis a monthly contribution is split three ways: a management fee to the operator, a donation to the Takaful fund for protection, and a savings part invested on Mudarabah and returned with profit at maturity (p. 422)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Tabarru‘ in Takaful is the same as Sadaqah, so no benefit can return to the donor.","right":"Not every donation is Sadaqah. A Waqf donor gives up individual ownership but may benefit as one of the fund’s beneficiaries (p. 423)."},{"wrong":"Early Takaful operators always separated underwriting surplus from investment profit.","right":"Early operators did not distinguish them. Later research concluded that the whole UWS/UWL belongs to participants, with the operator earning a fee and/or an investment-profit share (p. 423)."}],"relatedTopics":["t16.4.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In general Takaful, the participant's contribution is:","options":["Split into savings and protection","Wholly a donation for protection","A loan to the company","A deposit returned with interest"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 422–423."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch16-t4"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch16-t4","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"16.4","sectionTitle":"How the Takaful System Works","title":"How the Takaful System Works","covers":["t16.4","t16.4.1","t16.4.2","t16.4.3"],"overview":"A Takaful company acts as trustee/manager (Wakalah or Mudarabah basis) over a fund built from participant contributions, paying claims from the fund and distributing any underwriting surplus to participants.","simpleExplanation":"Participants pay into a shared fund; the Takaful company manages that fund either as an agent (Wakalah, earning a fee) or as a partner (Mudarabah, sharing profit); claims are paid out of the fund, and whatever is left over after claims - the underwriting surplus - belongs to the participants as a group, not the company, while any investment profit made on the fund is shared per the agreed basis.","academicExplanation":"A Takaful company serves as trustee or manager, on a Wakalah or Mudarabah basis, to operate the business. The operator and policy-taking participants contribute to the Takaful fund; claims are paid from this fund and the underwriting surplus or deficit is shared by the participants. In life (family) policies, part of the contribution is kept as an investment fund, with the operator using funds in business on a Wakalah or Mudarabah basis; the underwriting surplus/deficit belongs to the policyholders/partners, while investment-profit distribution depends on whether the Wakalah or Mudarabah basis applies. The modus operandi divides into family Takaful (life policies) and general Takaful. For family Takaful, the life policyholder's contribution splits into a 'protection part' (funding the Takaful fund/claims) and a savings/investment part if the company works as Mudarib; if the company works on a Wakalah basis, contributions split three ways - a management fee part, a protection part, and an investment part. The protection part operates on the donation (Tabarru') principle, with individual rights given up in favour of the Waqf; in the investment/savings part, individual rights remain intact under the Mudarabah principle, with contributions plus profit (net of expenses) paid to policyholders at the end of the policy term or earlier if required. For general Takaful, the whole contribution is treated as a protection donation, participants relinquish ownership rights in favour of the Takaful fund, and the UWS/UWL belongs to the participants; the company provides Qard al Hasan to the fund if claims exceed available funds/reserves at any point. Takaful companies can arrange re-Takaful on the same bases (Tabarru', Waqf, Mudarabah), paying an agreed contribution from the Takaful fund to a re-Takaful operator, who assists in case of losses. On the treatment of Tabarru': not every donation is equivalent to Sadaqah (charity, which can neither be taken back nor benefited from once given) - operators working on the Mudarabah model view the Takaful fund as a separate legal entity, with the protection-part contribution conditional on being used to pay claims, and any surplus potentially returned to participants (proportionate ownership of the contribution remains with participants to the extent funds are unused for claims). Where the amount is treated as Tabarru' from the outset (the Waqf model), it is used for the Waqf's beneficiaries; like Sadaqah, the donor relinquishes individual ownership, but unlike Sadaqah, the donor can still benefit as one of the beneficiaries - this is why the Waqf-based model, introduced more recently, is considered preferable. The whole contribution (or a part of it) becomes a donation to the Waqf fund; policyholders have no claim on the donation part used for claims payment, while the operator invests the funds and shares profits with the Waqf fund, and policyholders receive any profit share as fund beneficiaries. Historically, early Takaful operators made no distinction between underwriting surplus and 'investment profit', with surplus/profit sharing only after fulfilling the obligation of assisting fellow participants; ongoing scholarly research has settled that the whole UWS/UWL should belong to participants/policyholders, with the Takaful operator earning only a Takaful fee and/or a share of the 'investment profit'.","examExplanation":"Family Takaful contribution split: protection part (Tabarru' to Waqf/fund) + investment/savings part (Mudarabah, individual rights intact) [+ management fee part if Wakalah basis]. General Takaful: whole contribution = donation for protection; UWS/UWL belongs to participants; Qard al Hasan from company if claims exceed fund. Modern consensus: whole UWS/UWL belongs to participants; operator earns only a fee and/or investment-profit share, not a share of underwriting surplus.","keyPoints":["A Takaful company operates as trustee/manager on a Wakalah or Mudarabah basis, with the underwriting surplus/deficit belonging to the participants","Family Takaful contributions split into a protection part (Tabarru') and an investment/savings part (Mudarabah); Wakalah-basis contracts also carve out a management fee part","In general Takaful, the whole contribution is a donation for protection, and participants relinquish ownership rights in favour of the fund","The Takaful company provides Qard al Hasan to the fund if claims exceed available funds and reserves","Re-Takaful (reinsurance for Takaful companies) operates on the same Tabarru', Waqf and Mudarabah bases","The settled scholarly consensus is that the whole underwriting surplus/deficit belongs to participants, while the operator earns only a fee and/or a share of investment profit"],"definitions":[{"term":"Family Takaful","definition":"The Takaful equivalent of life insurance, where the participant's contribution is split into a protection part (Tabarru' to the Takaful fund) and an investment/savings part managed on a Mudarabah basis."},{"term":"General Takaful","definition":"Takaful cover for non-life risks, where the whole contribution is treated as a donation (Tabarru') for protection, with the underwriting surplus or deficit belonging to the participants as a group."},{"term":"Investment Profit (in Takaful)","definition":"Profit earned by the Takaful operator investing fund assets in Shari'ah-compliant avenues, distinct from the underwriting surplus/deficit (UWS/UWL) generated from claims versus contributions."}],"conditions":["The company must provide Qard al Hasan to the fund if claims at any time exceed the amount available in it and reserves are insufficient"],"principles":["The whole underwriting surplus or deficit (UWS/UWL) belongs to the participants/policyholders as a group, not the operator","Under the Waqf model, the donor participant can still benefit as one of the beneficiaries of the fund, unlike ordinary Sadaqah"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Assuming the underwriting surplus in Takaful belongs to the operator/company, as it effectively does in conventional insurance - it belongs to the participants as a group under the settled scholarly consensus"],"distinctions":["Underwriting surplus/deficit (UWS/UWL, belongs to participants) versus investment profit (shared per Wakalah/Mudarabah terms between operator and fund/participants)","Tabarru' treated as immediately-relinquished Sadaqah versus Tabarru' treated as conditional on claims use, with surplus potentially returned to participants"],"relatedConcepts":["Tabarru'","Waqf","Wakalah","Mudarabah","Qard al Hasan","Re-Takaful"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4","pages":[422,423]},"transactionSteps":[{"step":"Participant pays contribution","description":"The participant pays a contribution to the Takaful company; in family Takaful this is divided into a protection part and a savings/investment part (plus a management fee part under a Wakalah basis); in general Takaful the whole contribution is treated as a donation for protection."},{"step":"Protection part donated to the Takaful fund (Tabarru')","description":"The participant relinquishes, as Tabarru', the protection portion of the contribution, with individual ownership rights given up in favour of the Takaful fund (structured as a Waqf in the Waqf-based model) to enable mutual help among participants."},{"step":"Operator manages the fund on a Wakalah or Mudarabah basis","description":"The Takaful company, acting as trustee/manager, administers the fund and invests it in Shari'ah-compliant avenues - as agent (Wakalah, earning a pre-agreed fee) or as Mudarib (Mudarabah, sharing investment profit on an agreed ratio)."},{"step":"Risk pooling across participants","description":"Losses of the unfortunate few are met from the contributions of the fortunate many exposed to the same risk, since the fund pools contributions from all participants on a cooperative risk-sharing basis."},{"step":"Claims paid from the Takaful fund","description":"When a participant suffers a defined loss, claims and related operational/re-Takaful costs are paid out of the Takaful fund; if claims exceed available funds and reserves, the operator provides Qard al Hasan (an interest-free loan) to the fund."},{"step":"Underwriting surplus or deficit and investment profit distributed","description":"Any underwriting surplus/deficit (UWS/UWL) belongs to the participants as a group and may be distributed to beneficiaries, reserved, or reinvested for the fund's benefit; investment profit is shared between the operator and the fund/participants per the agreed Wakalah or Mudarabah terms."}],"calculations":[{"title":"Practice Example — generated for learning: Wakalah/Mudarabah Family Takaful Contribution, Surplus, and Investment-Profit Split","formula":"Contribution = Management fee part + Tabarru' (protection) part + Investment/savings part. Underwriting surplus (UWS) = Total Tabarru' fund - Claims paid, shared among contributing participants. Investment profit share = Participant's invested balance x return rate x agreed Mudarabah ratio.","inputs":[{"label":"Participant's annual contribution (Wakalah-basis family Takaful)","value":"$1,000"},{"label":"Split: management fee part (to the operator, Wakalah fee)","value":"20% = $200"},{"label":"Split: protection part (Tabarru' to the Takaful fund)","value":"30% = $300"},{"label":"Split: investment/savings part (Mudarabah)","value":"50% = $500"},{"label":"Pool size (for the fund-level surplus calculation)","value":"1,000 participants, each contributing $300 Tabarru'"},{"label":"Total claims paid by the fund during the year","value":"$210,000"},{"label":"Assumed annual investment return on the invested part","value":"8%"},{"label":"Agreed Mudarabah profit-sharing ratio (participant : operator)","value":"60% : 40%"}],"steps":["Split the $1,000 contribution per the Wakalah-basis three-way division: $200 management fee (operator keeps this regardless of outcome), $300 Tabarru' (protection), $500 investment/savings.","At the fund level, 1,000 participants each donate $300 as Tabarru': total Takaful (protection) fund for the year = 1,000 x $300 = $300,000.","Claims paid out of the fund during the year = $210,000, leaving an underwriting surplus (UWS) = $300,000 - $210,000 = $90,000, which belongs to the participants as a group, not the operator.","If the $90,000 UWS is distributed pro rata to the 1,000 contributing participants, each participant's share = $90,000 / 1,000 = $90.","Separately, the participant's own $500 investment/savings part earns the assumed 8% annual return: $500 x 8% = $40 investment profit for the year.","Under the agreed 60:40 Mudarabah ratio, the participant keeps 60% of this profit: $40 x 60% = $24, and the operator (as Mudarib) keeps 40%: $40 x 40% = $16.","For this one participant, total value returned beyond the $200 fee already paid = $90 (UWS share) + $24 (investment profit share) = $114, on top of the $500 savings principal which remains in their investment account."],"result":"Each participant receives a $90 underwriting-surplus share (from the $300,000 Tabarru' fund less $210,000 in claims) plus a $24 investment-profit share (60% of the $40 earned on their $500 savings balance) - a total of $114 returned to them for the year, while the operator earns the $200 Wakalah fee plus its $16 (40%) share of investment profit.","interpretation":"The book's description of how Takaful works (16.4) is conceptual rather than numeric, so every dollar figure here is invented for practice; the mechanism it illustrates is real: the underwriting surplus belongs entirely to participants as a group (never the operator, under the settled modern consensus described in the text), while investment profit is a separate pool split per the agreed Wakalah/Mudarabah terms - which is exactly why the book insists on keeping UWS/UWL and 'investment profit' analytically distinct even though early Takaful operators historically blurred the two.","generated":true,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4","pages":[422,423]}}]}]},{"id":"t16.4.1","section":"16.4.1","title":"Models of Takaful (Box 16.1)","pages":[423,426],"tier":"core","concepts":["takaful","wakalah","mudarabah","waqf"],"intuition":"Which model keeps the operator from being a risk-taker?","simpleExplanation":"Pure Wakalah (Middle East): operator is agent for a fee (e.g. 30% of donations); UWS/UWL and investment profit belong to participants; the operator may get a performance share of UWS but never bears UWL — scholars have reservations about equity. Pure Mudarabah (Asia–Pacific): profit (investment return plus UWS) shared, e.g. 50:50 — serious objections since Tabarru‘ cannot be Mudarabah capital and the operator takes UWS without bearing UWL. Wakalah–Mudarabah: fee plus shares of UWS and investment profit — structural problems. Waqf / Wakalah–Waqf: shareholders create a Waqf fund with an initial donation; contributions go partly to the fund (donation) and partly to investment; UWS/UWL belong to the fund; Mudarabah between fund and company for investments. Research by over forty scholars led by Mufti Taqi Usmani favours Waqf or Wakalah–Waqf.","academicExplanation":["Wakalah fee fixed annually in advance with the Shari’ah committee; operator provides Qard al Hasan for deficits.","Mudarabah model: sharing ratio approved annually; most expenses charged to shareholders.","Waqf model: multiple funds for classes of business; operational costs (re-Takaful, claims) from the fund; no obligation to distribute surplus; rules on fees, profit, reserves and compensation set beforehand; investment part redeemed on NAV basis.","Box 16.1 (Wakalah with Waqf): shareholders create the Waqf; participants donate; company takes 25–30% as management fee (bearing management expenses), 70–75% invested; claims and re-Takaful from the fund; UWS/UWL to participants as a group; Mudarabah profit split 40% company : 60% fund (example ratio). Company income: Waqf management fee, Mudarib share or agency charge, and profit on shareholders' funds; the fund may keep a contingency reserve."],"examExplanation":"Models: Wakalah (fee; UWS/UWL to participants; operator performance share questioned), Mudarabah (UWS shared, operator not bearing UWL — objectionable), Wakalah–Mudarabah (structural issues), Waqf/Wakalah–Waqf (preferred; Usmani-led research). Box 16.1: 25–30% fee; 70–75% invested; Mudarabah 40:60.","keyPoints":["Operator should not be a risk-taker.","Qard al Hasan covers fund deficits.","The Waqf fund may hold a contingency reserve from contributions and investment profit; the reserve belongs to the Waqf (pp. 425–426).","In the Waqf model there is no obligation to distribute the surplus. It may be distributed, reserved or reinvested for the Waqf (p. 424).","Company income in the Waqf model comes from the management fee, the Mudarib share or investment-agent charge, and profit on shareholders’ money (p. 426)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Pure Wakalah fee","kind":"textbook","text":"An operator charges a Wakalah fee of 30 % of donations and may take a performance share of any surplus. If there is a deficit, participants bear it and the operator only lends Qard al Hasan — hence scholars’ reservations about equity (p. 424)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t16.4.2","t16.4.3","t13.2.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which model did research by over forty scholars under Mufti Taqi Usmani favour?","options":["Pure Mudarabah","Waqf or Wakalah–Waqf","Conventional mutual","Wakalah–Mudarabah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 423."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch16-t5"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch16-t5","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"16.4.1","sectionTitle":"Models of Takaful","title":"Models of Takaful: Wakalah, Mudarabah, Waqf and Wakalah-Waqf","covers":["t16.4.1"],"overview":"Scholars have proposed several Takaful operating models, with the Wakalah-Waqf combination - per Shaikh Muhammad Taqi Usmani's research - identified as the best basis for a practical, Shari'ah-compliant Takaful system.","simpleExplanation":"Different regions run Takaful differently - the Middle East mostly uses a pure Wakalah (agency-fee) model, Asia-Pacific mostly uses a pure Mudarabah (profit-sharing) model, and there are combined Mudarabah-Wakalah models too - but after extensive research, over forty scholars concluded that a Waqf-based model, or a combination of Wakalah and Waqf, works best because it avoids most of the fairness problems the other models have.","academicExplanation":"Per the latest research by over forty Shari'ah scholars under the guidance of Shaikh Muhammad Taqi Usmani (a renowned contemporary jurist, member of the Shari'ah councils of the OIC/IDB and AAOIFI), a Waqf model or a combination of Wakalah and Waqf is the best basis for a practical Shari'ah-compliant Takaful system; some jurists had earlier advocated a Waqf mechanism even before this research. In a pure Wakalah model (generally practised in the Middle East), the operator acts as Wakil for participants and earns a fee as an agreed percentage (e.g. 30%) of participants' donations, with the whole UWS/UWL and investment profit/loss belonging to the policyholders/participants; the Wakalah fee covers all management expenses and is fixed annually in advance with the company's Shari'ah committee; a part of UWS may be given to the operator as a performance incentive, though UWL is borne only by participants (the operator simply provides Qard al Hasan) - a feature Shari'ah scholars have found inequitable. In a pure Mudarabah model (mainly practised in Asia-Pacific), participants and the operator enter a Mudarabah contract for cooperative loss-sharing and profit-sharing; profit (investment returns plus any underwriting surplus) is distributed per a mutually agreed ratio (e.g. 50:50, 60:40) approved annually by the Shari'ah committee, with most expenses charged to shareholders. This model has an issue: Tabarru'-donated amounts cannot simultaneously serve as Mudarabah capital, and the operator gets UWS but does not bear UWL, prompting serious Shari'ah objections. A combined Mudarabah-Wakalah model shares both UWS and investment profit between participants and operators as a contractual entitlement, but has a structural issue: under Mudarabah, the operator (as Mudarib) cannot charge management expenses separately from its Mudarib share, whereas under Wakalah the operator (as agent) can take management fees from the fund per pre-agreed terms - and the operator still does not bear UWL, so this model also faces Shari'ah concerns. In the Waqf model, shareholders create a Waqf fund (Takaful fund) via an initial donation; more than one fund can exist for different service classes. Participant contributions (risk-appropriate) split into a donation to the Takaful fund and an investment portion on a Mudarabah basis; the donation part always remains with the Waqf, and operational costs (re-Takaful, claims) are met from the fund. The underwriting surplus/loss belongs to the fund, which may be distributed to Waqf beneficiaries, kept as reserve, or reinvested - there is no obligation to distribute the surplus. Management fees, profit distribution rules, reserve creation, and compensation extent/limits are all decided beforehand; shareholders provide Qard al Hasan to the fund if needed. A Mudarabah contract between the Takaful fund and the company (as Mudarib) governs investment, with the investment part redeemed to the policyholder on a NAV basis at policy maturity, and investment profit shared between company and fund. Box 16.1 details the Wakalah-with-Waqf model flowchart: (1) shareholders create a Waqf fund via initial donation; policyholders/participants then donate to the fund; (2) the company invests 70-75% of the Waqf fund on a Wakalah basis, with 25-30% retained as its management fee to cover all management expenses; (3) claims/operational costs of Takaful and re-Takaful are charged to the fund, with UWS/UWL belonging to participants as a group; (4) a Mudarabah contract for investment purposes operates between the fund and company, with an example profit-sharing ratio of 60% (fund) to 40% (company), though this can be any agreed ratio. Besides technical reserves, the Waqf fund may create a contingency reserve fund from contributions and investment profit, itself Waqf property; the company's income sources are the Waqf management fee, a share of investment profit as Mudarib or a service charge as investment agent, and profit from shareholders' own money.","examExplanation":"Pure Wakalah model (Middle East): operator gets agreed % fee (e.g. 30%) of donations; UWS/UWL fully belongs to participants; operator only provides Qard al Hasan on deficit (criticized as inequitable since operator gets performance incentive from UWS but bears no UWL). Pure Mudarabah model (Asia-Pacific): profit (investment + UWS) shared per agreed ratio (e.g. 50:50); issue - Tabarru' can't simultaneously be Mudarabah capital, and operator gets UWS without bearing UWL. Waqf/Wakalah-Waqf model: recommended by 40+ scholars under Usmani; Box 16.1 example - 70-75% invested on Wakalah basis (25-30% fee), Mudarabah investment split e.g. 60:40 (fund:company).","keyPoints":["Over forty Shari'ah scholars under Shaikh Muhammad Taqi Usmani's guidance identified a Waqf model or Wakalah-Waqf combination as the best basis for practical Takaful","Pure Wakalah model: operator earns an agreed percentage fee of donations; participants bear the full UWS/UWL, with the operator only providing Qard al Hasan on deficit","Pure Mudarabah model: operator shares in UWS but does not bear UWL, and Tabarru'-donated funds cannot simultaneously serve as Mudarabah capital - both raising Shari'ah objections","The combined Mudarabah-Wakalah model has a structural issue: Mudarib cannot separately charge management expenses, while Wakil can - creating inconsistency","In the Waqf model, shareholders create the fund via an initial donation; there is no obligation to distribute any underwriting surplus","In the Box 16.1 Wakalah-Waqf example, the company invests 70-75% of the fund on a Wakalah basis (retaining 25-30% as management fee), with investment profit shared via a separate Mudarabah contract (e.g. 60:40 fund-to-company)"],"definitions":[{"term":"Wakalah Model (Takaful)","definition":"A Takaful operating model, mainly practised in the Middle East, where the operator acts as Wakil (agent) for participants, earning a fixed percentage fee of contributions, while the whole underwriting surplus/deficit and investment profit/loss belong to the participants."},{"term":"Mudarabah Model (Takaful)","definition":"A Takaful operating model, mainly practised in Asia-Pacific, where the operator and participants enter a Mudarabah contract, sharing both investment returns and underwriting surplus per an agreed ratio, though the operator does not bear underwriting losses."},{"term":"Waqf Model (Takaful)","definition":"A Takaful operating model where shareholders create a Waqf (endowment) fund via an initial donation; participant contributions are then donated to this fund, with the underwriting surplus or loss belonging to the fund, and no obligation to distribute any surplus."}],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Wakalah-Waqf Flowchart (Box 16.1)","kind":"textbook","text":"Shareholders create a Waqf fund via an initial donation; the company invests 70-75% of the fund on a Wakalah basis, retaining 25-30% as its management fee; claims/re-Takaful costs are charged to the fund with UWS/UWL belonging to participants; a separate Mudarabah contract splits investment profit, e.g. 60% to the fund and 40% to the company."}],"commonConfusions":["Assuming all Takaful models are equally Shari'ah-uncontroversial - the pure Mudarabah and pure Wakalah models both face documented scholarly objections, unlike the Waqf/Wakalah-Waqf model"],"distinctions":["Pure Wakalah (operator earns a fee, no UWL risk-sharing) versus pure Mudarabah (operator shares UWS but still doesn't bear UWL) versus Waqf/Wakalah-Waqf (fund-centric, no distribution obligation on surplus)"],"relatedConcepts":["Wakalah","Mudarabah","Waqf","Tabarru'","Shaikh Muhammad Taqi Usmani"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","pages":[423,424,425,426]}}],"processSteps":["Box 16.1 Wakalah–Waqf flow (p. 425): shareholders make an initial donation to create the Waqf fund, and participants donate to it","The company keeps 25–30 % as its management fee and invests 70–75 % of the fund","Claims and re-Takaful costs are charged to the fund; UWS/UWL belongs to participants as a group","Investment profit is shared by Mudarabah between fund and company (e.g. 60:40)"],"table":{"caption":"Takaful models compared (pp. 423–425)","head":["Model","Where practised","Operator income","Concern"],"rows":[["Pure Wakalah","Middle East","Fixed % fee (e.g. 30 %); sometimes a performance share of UWS","UWL borne only by participants; operator gives only Qard; seen as inequitable"],["Pure Mudarabah","Asia–Pacific","Share of investment profit plus UWS (e.g. 50:50)","A donation cannot be Mudarabah capital; takes UWS but not UWL"],["Wakalah–Mudarabah","Some companies","Fee plus shares of UWS and investment profit","A Mudarib cannot also charge fees; no UWL borne"],["Waqf / Wakalah–Waqf","Recent, recommended","Management fee plus Mudarib share of investment profit","Preferred by Usmani-led research of 40+ scholars"]]},"calc":{"type":"takaful-waqf","note":"Reproduces Box 16.1's illustrative split: enter total donations, claims and investment return; shows the 25–30% operator fee, the invested portion, the fund's underwriting result and the 60:40 Mudarabah profit split. Other ratios are practice inputs."}},{"id":"t16.4.2","section":"16.4.2","title":"Issues in the Mudarabah Model","pages":[426,426],"tier":"core","concepts":["takaful","mudarabah"],"intuition":"Why is Mudarabah good for deposits but bad for insurance?","simpleExplanation":"Four objections: (1) participants' relationship should rest on Tabarru‘, not Mudarabah — a donation cannot simultaneously be Mudarabah capital; (2) sharing UWS makes Takaful like insurance, and worse, since operators take UWS but bear no UWL — the operator becomes a risk-taker; (3) Mudarabah capital must be returned with profit or reduced by loss, whereas non-life contributions are not returned; (4) Qard al Hasan from the operator contradicts Mudarabah, and a Mudarib cannot guarantee the financier.","academicExplanation":["Some operators charge management expenses to the Takaful fund — against Mudarabah rules — and take a performance share of UWS.","The author considers Mudarabah highly suitable for Islamic banking, especially deposits, but not for insurance (p. 426). In the Mudarabah model, contributions and investment income pay claims and re-Takaful costs. Shareholders should meet management and marketing costs from their share, but some operators charge these to the fund, which breaks Mudarabah rules. Operators may also take part of the underwriting surplus (p. 426). The book lists four objections. The cooperative nature is undermined, since a donation cannot also be Mudarabah capital. Sharing the UWS makes the operator a risk-taker, and one that takes surplus without bearing deficit. Mudarabah capital must be returned with profit or reduced by loss, whereas non-life contributions are never returned. And a Mudarib who must provide Qard al Hasan is acting as guarantor, which contradicts Mudarabah (p. 426)."],"examExplanation":"Mudarabah model issues: donation ≠ capital; operator takes UWS without UWL; capital not returned in non-life; Qard/guarantee inconsistent; expenses charged to fund.","keyPoints":["An operator should not be a risk-taker."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Operator takes surplus, not deficit","kind":"practice","text":"Year 1: surplus 4 m, the operator takes half under a Mudarabah model. Year 2: deficit 3 m, participants bear it and the operator only lends. This one-sided pattern is exactly what scholars object to (p. 426)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A Mudarabah Takaful operator may charge management expenses to the Takaful fund in addition to its profit share.","right":"Under Mudarabah rules, management costs come from the operator’s own share, not the fund (p. 426)."}],"relatedTopics":["t16.4.1","t12.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why is a donation (Tabarru‘) problematic as Mudarabah capital?","options":["It is too small","Donated funds cannot simultaneously be capital to be returned with profit","It is taxed","Only banks can donate"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 426."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch16-t6"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch16-t6","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"16.4.2-16.4.3","sectionTitle":"Issues in the Mudarabah Model; Issues in Wakalah and Wakalah-Mudarabah Models","title":"Shari'ah Issues in the Mudarabah, Wakalah and Wakalah-Mudarabah Models","covers":["t16.4.2","t16.4.3"],"overview":"Shari'ah scholars have raised specific objections to the Mudarabah, Wakalah-with-Shirkah and combined models, mainly around the operator sharing in underwriting surplus without bearing underwriting loss.","simpleExplanation":"Scholars point out real design flaws in the popular models: in the Mudarabah model, the company gets a share of the surplus but never has to cover a deficit, which isn't fair profit-sharing at all; similar unfairness shows up when Wakalah is combined with profit-sharing arrangements, since a genuine risk-pooling scheme requires the surplus to stay with the pool members, not be shared with the manager.","academicExplanation":"While Mudarabah is highly suitable for Islamic banking (especially deposits), it is not well suited to the insurance business. In a Mudarabah Takaful model, participant contributions and investment income fund claims, re-Takaful costs and related expenses from the general Takaful fund; shareholders normally meet management/marketing expenses from their own share, retaining any remainder as net profit - though some companies improperly charge management expenses to the Takaful fund itself, against Mudarabah rules; a portion of any underwriting surplus is also given to the operator based on performance. Scholars raise several specific objections: (1) the arrangement undermines the cooperative nature of the contract - the participant relationship should rest on Tabarru', not Mudarabah, since profit-sharing logic does not apply and a donation cannot simultaneously be Mudarabah capital; (2) sharing in any UWS makes the Takaful contract essentially resemble conventional insurance, where shareholders become risk-takers (getting UWS or bearing UWL) - the Mudarabah-based model is arguably worse, since operators/shareholders take only the UWS without bearing the UWL, meaning a Takaful operator should not be a risk-taker in the way this model makes them; (3) in Mudarabah, invested capital must be returned with any profit, and losses are deducted from capital, but in non-life Takaful paid premiums/contributions are simply not returned - a structural mismatch; (4) the requirement to provide Qard al Hasan in case of a fund deficit is itself against the definition of Mudarabah as a pure profit-sharing contract, and a Mudarib cannot act as guarantor to the financier (the fund). On Wakalah and Wakalah-Mudarabah models: Wakalah combined with Shirkah arrangements (as in most Middle Eastern Takaful companies giving shareholders a part of any UWS) faces the same objections as the Mudarabah model - the problem arising specifically when the operator receives a part of UWS in addition to its operating fee as a performance incentive, when surplus-sharing should properly remain among the pool members/fund. Additional recommendations: the risk premium should be separately defined and related to the actual risk, remaining the same for similar risks regardless of the client's identity; for large clients, the company should reduce the operator's fee rather than the risk premium rate; and expenses related to initial company set-up should be borne by the shareholders, not the Takaful fund.","examExplanation":"Mudarabah model objections: (1) cooperative Tabarru' relationship undermined by Mudarabah profit-sharing logic; (2) operator gets UWS but not UWL - worse than conventional insurance's risk-taking; (3) invested Mudarabah capital should be returned, but non-life premiums are not; (4) Qard al Hasan requirement on deficit contradicts pure Mudarabah definition. Wakalah+Shirkah objection: same UWS-without-UWL problem when operator gets part of surplus as performance incentive alongside its fee.","keyPoints":["A donation (Tabarru') cannot simultaneously serve as Mudarabah capital - this undermines the cooperative nature the Takaful contract should have","In the Mudarabah model, the operator/shareholders take a share of underwriting surplus (UWS) but do not bear underwriting loss (UWL) - considered even worse than conventional insurance's risk allocation","Non-life Takaful premiums are not returned to participants, unlike Mudarabah capital, which must be returned along with any profit","Requiring the operator to provide Qard al Hasan on a deficit is itself inconsistent with the pure profit-sharing definition of Mudarabah","Wakalah-with-Shirkah models face the same core objection when the operator receives part of the underwriting surplus as a performance incentive on top of its fee","Recommended fixes: risk premium should be uniform for similar risks regardless of client identity, fee (not risk premium) should be reduced for large clients, and set-up expenses should be borne by shareholders"],"definitions":[],"conditions":["Risk premium should be separately defined and related to the risk, remaining the same for similar risks regardless of the client","For large clients, the company should reduce the operator's fee, not the risk premium rate","Expenses related to initial company set-up should be borne by the shareholders, not the Takaful fund"],"principles":["A Takaful operator should not be a risk-taker who benefits from underwriting surplus without also bearing underwriting loss"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Assuming any model where the operator earns a performance-linked share of surplus is automatically fine, when the core Shari'ah objection is specifically that surplus-sharing without loss-sharing turns the operator into an inappropriate risk-taker"],"distinctions":["A donation-based Tabarru' relationship (cooperative) versus a Mudarabah profit-sharing relationship (commercial) - the two logics conflict when combined without careful structuring"],"relatedConcepts":["Mudarabah","Wakalah","Shirkah","Tabarru'","Qard al Hasan","Underwriting surplus/deficit"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.2-16.4.3","pages":[426,427]}}]},{"id":"t16.4.3","section":"16.4.3","title":"Issues in Wakalah and Wakalah–Mudarabah Models","pages":[426,426],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["takaful","wakalah"],"intuition":"How should pricing and surplus sharing work fairly?","simpleExplanation":"Wakalah combined with Shirkah (giving shareholders part of UWS as performance incentive) faces the same objections as Mudarabah — surplus should be shared among pool members. The risk premium should be defined separately, related to risk and the same for similar risks regardless of client; for large clients reduce the operator's fee, not the risk premium; initial set-up expenses should be borne by shareholders.","academicExplanation":["Most Middle Eastern Takaful companies give part of UWS to shareholders.","Wakalah combined with Shirkah, where operators get part of the UWS as a performance incentive on top of their fee, faces the same objection as Mudarabah. The surplus should be shared only among pool members (p. 426). The book also sets out pricing principles. The risk premium should be defined separately and relate to the risk, the same for similar risks whoever the client is. For large clients, the operator should reduce its fees, not the risk premium. Initial set-up costs should be borne by shareholders (p. 426)."],"examExplanation":"Wakalah–Shirkah issues: UWS to operator; risk premium separate and uniform; discount fees not risk premium; set-up costs on shareholders.","keyPoints":["Surplus sharing belongs to pool members."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Discount for a large client","kind":"textbook","text":"A corporate client insures a large fleet. Rather than lowering the risk premium below that charged to others with the same risk, the operator should reduce its own fee (p. 426)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Giving the Wakalah operator a share of UWS as a performance bonus raises no Shari’ah issue.","right":"It draws the same objection as the Mudarabah model: the surplus should go only to pool members (p. 426)."}],"relatedTopics":["t16.4.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"For a large client, what should a Takaful operator reduce?","options":["The risk premium rate","The operator's fee","Claims payable","The Waqf corpus"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 426."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t16.5","section":"16.5","title":"Takaful and Conventional Insurance Compared","pages":[427,428],"tier":"core","concepts":["takaful","insurance"],"intuition":"Where does the surplus go — and what does that do to pricing?","simpleExplanation":"Conventional insurance transfers risk to the company and involves Riba, Gharar and gambling; Takaful participants donate to a fund that bears losses and share UWS/UWL, with remaining uncertainty inside the group, cushioned by reserves and shareholder Qard al Hasan. Conventional premiums are profit-driven; Takaful premiums aim at adequacy. Surplus returns to participants, countering overpricing. Life policies: conventional rests on Riba and nominees; family Takaful on Waqf, Tabarru‘ and Mudarabah, with benefits to heirs by inheritance and wills; agents paid by the company. Investments: Takaful only Shari’ah-compliant.","academicExplanation":["Losses are absorbed first by reserves, then interest-free loans from shareholders, then general price increases.","Family Takaful payouts: on death before maturity — investment part with profit, amount from the Takaful fund and discretionary company donation; on survival — investment part with profit, pro rata share in UWS and bonus; on early termination — investment part with profit and reduced share of UWS, but no fund assistance."],"examExplanation":"Compare: risk transfer vs mutual sharing; premium profit-driven vs adequacy; UWS to company vs participants; life: nominee/interest vs heirs/Waqf-Mudarabah; investments: any vs compliant only.","keyPoints":["Built-in check against overpricing.","Some uncertainty remains, but it is within the group, which is why the model where UWS/UWL fully belongs to participants is considered best (p. 427).","Takaful has a built-in check on overpricing, since surplus returns to participants in proportion to contributions (p. 427)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Absorbing a deficit","kind":"textbook","text":"A Takaful fund ends the year with claims exceeding contributions. The deficit is met first from reserves, then by an interest-free loan from shareholders, and only then by a general increase in pricing (p. 427)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A family Takaful participant who terminates early gets help from the Takaful fund.","right":"On early termination he gets his investment part with profit and a reduced pro rata UWS share, but no assistance from the fund (p. 428)."}],"relatedTopics":["t16.2.1","t16.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In family Takaful, the insurable interest on death vests in:","options":["A nominee of choice irrespective of Shari’ah","The assured's heirs according to inheritance and wills","The company","The agent"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 427."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch16-t7"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch16-t7","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"16.5","sectionTitle":"Takaful and Conventional Insurance Compared","title":"Takaful and Conventional Insurance Compared","covers":["t16.5"],"overview":"Takaful and conventional insurance differ fundamentally in objectives, risk transfer, investment policy and life-policy treatment, with Takaful's participant-owned surplus acting as a built-in check on overpricing.","simpleExplanation":"In conventional insurance, you pay a premium and the company takes on the risk, keeping any profit for itself; in Takaful, the risk stays within the group of participants who pool their money, and any leftover surplus goes back to them - which naturally discourages Takaful companies from overcharging, since they don't keep the extra profit the way an insurer would.","academicExplanation":"Takaful and conventional insurance differ in objectives, structure, investment policies and returns. In conventional insurance, risk transfers to one party (the company), and Riba, Gharar and gambling are involved; policyholders pay premiums against unknown risks (general insurance), while life insurance policyholders get back premiums plus interest on survival, or the insured amount on death before maturity. In Takaful, participants relinquish ownership of their donation amount, with the Waqf fund bearing losses to any member and members sharing UWS/UWL; the company manages the business and shares investment profit with policyholders. Some uncertainty remains, but it stays within the group itself, since all members have jointly contributed to help those who incur loss and share the remainder - this is why the model giving UWS/UWL fully to participants is considered the best per the latest research; uncertainty is further minimized via reserves and access to Qard al Hasan from shareholders when needed. The risk premium in conventional insurance is commercially driven, aiming at maximum shareholder profit; in Takaful, premium adequacy is the main consideration, with the profit element subject to equity, justice and ethics. Losses (underwriting or investment) are first absorbed by reserves, then by interest-free shareholder loans, and only then by a general pricing increase - giving Takaful a built-in mechanism against overpricing, since whatever the premium amount, surplus returns to participants in proportion to their contributions. On life policies specifically: conventional life insurance revolves around Riba, while the Islamic model rests mainly on Waqf, Tabarru' and Mudarabah; under conventional policies, agents are paid from the assureds' premiums, while under family Takaful, agents work for and are paid by the company itself. Regarding insurable interest: under conventional systems it vests in the policyholder if alive at expiry, or the nominee if the assured dies within the period; under the Islamic model, insurable interest vests in the assured or their heirs per inheritance/will principles. On death before maturity, conventional insurance pays the nominee the whole agreed amount; Takaful instead gives the beneficiary the investment part plus profit, any Takaful fund amount, and a company donation at its discretion. If alive at maturity, conventional insurance pays the whole agreed amount plus interest/dividends/bonus; Takaful gives the investment part plus profit, a pro rata UWS share, and a dividend/bonus per company policy. On premature termination, Takaful gives the investment part plus profit and a pro rata UWS share (with reduced weightages by years elapsed), but no assistance from the Takaful fund. Perhaps the most visible distinction is fund investment: insurance companies invest without regard to Halal/Haram, including interest-based avenues, while Takaful companies undertake only Shari'ah-compliant business, distributing profits per the pre-agreed Takaful agreement formula.","examExplanation":"Key contrasts: conventional = risk transferred to insurer, insurer keeps profit; Takaful = risk pooled among participants, surplus returned to them. Loss absorption order in Takaful: reserves -> interest-free shareholder loans -> general price increase (a built-in anti-overpricing mechanism). Conventional life insurance = Riba-based, nominee gets full amount + interest; Takaful = Waqf/Tabarru'/Mudarabah-based, beneficiary gets investment part + profit + fund amount + discretionary company donation.","keyPoints":["In conventional insurance, risk transfers fully to the insurer; in Takaful, risk is pooled among participants via the Waqf fund","The Takaful model giving the whole UWS/UWL to participants is considered the best per the latest scholarly research","Takaful has a built-in anti-overpricing mechanism: losses are absorbed first by reserves, then interest-free shareholder loans, then a general pricing increase - with surplus always returning to participants","Under conventional life insurance, agents are paid from the assured's premiums; under family Takaful, agents are paid by the company itself","Insurable interest under the Islamic model vests in the assured or their heirs per inheritance/will principles, rather than a designated nominee","Takaful companies invest only in Shari'ah-compliant avenues, unlike conventional insurers who invest without regard to Halal/Haram"],"definitions":[],"conditions":[],"principles":["In Takaful, premium adequacy (not shareholder profit maximization) is the main pricing consideration, subject to equity, justice and ethics"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Assuming Takaful eliminates uncertainty entirely - some uncertainty remains, but it stays within the participant group itself rather than being transferred to a risk-taking third party"],"distinctions":["Conventional insurance's loss-absorption path (borne entirely by the insurer/shareholders) versus Takaful's ordered path (reserves -> interest-free shareholder loans -> pricing adjustment, with surplus ultimately belonging to participants)"],"relatedConcepts":["Waqf","Tabarru'","Mudarabah","Underwriting surplus/deficit","Qard al Hasan"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.5","pages":[427,428]}}],"processSteps":["How losses are absorbed (p. 427): first the reserves","Then interest-free loans (Qard) from shareholders","Then a general increase in pricing by the company"],"table":{"caption":"Takaful vs conventional insurance (pp. 427–428)","head":["Aspect","Conventional","Takaful"],"rows":[["Risk","Transferred to the company","Shared within the group via the Waqf/fund"],["UWS/UWL","Company’s","Participants’"],["Premium","Commercially driven to maximise profit","Adequacy the main concern; profit subject to equity and ethics"],["Investments","Includes interest-based and Haram avenues","Shari’ah-compliant only"],["Agents paid from","Assureds’ premiums","The company’s own funds"],["Insurable interest","Nominee on death","Assured or heirs by inheritance and wills"],["Life maturity","Sum plus interest, dividends, bonus","Investment part plus profit, UWS share, bonus"]]}},{"id":"t16.6","section":"16.6","title":"Status and Potential of the Takaful Industry","pages":[428,429],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["takaful"],"intuition":"Why has Takaful lagged behind Islamic banking?","simpleExplanation":"First company: Islamic Insurance Company of Sudan (1979); Malaysia from 1984. Growth 10–20% p.a. vs about 5% for global insurance; over 60 companies in 24 countries; re-Takaful in Malaysia, Bahrain, Saudi Arabia and UAE. It has lagged banking because of the huge investment needed and required regulatory changes. Potential: low insurance density and penetration in Muslim countries, growth of Ijarah and home finance, motor, health and family needs. Islamic banks might jointly establish well-capitalised Takaful companies.","academicExplanation":["Only a few companies offer comprehensive family Takaful (e.g. Bank Aljazira's Takaful Ta‘awani); awareness, particularly of family Takaful, is key.","Takaful has proved viable within about two decades. The first company was the Islamic Insurance Company of Sudan in 1979, and Malaysia followed in 1984. The industry has grown at 10–20 % a year against about 5 % for insurance globally. There are over 60 companies (including windows) in 24 countries, and re-Takaful exists in Malaysia, Bahrain, Saudi Arabia and the UAE (p. 428). Takaful has lagged behind Islamic banking because of the heavy investment needed to compete and the regulatory changes needed for a level playing field, as Malaysia’s experience shows (p. 428). Potential is large. Insurance density and penetration are low in Muslim countries because many believe insurance is un-Islamic, while Islamic Ijarah and home finance create demand. Bank Aljazira was cited as the only exclusive family Takaful provider in the Middle East. The author suggests Islamic banks jointly set up well-capitalised Takaful companies where none exist (pp. 428–429)."],"examExplanation":"1979 Sudan; 1984 Malaysia; growth 10–20%; >60 companies, 24 countries; lag reasons: investment, regulation; potential: low density/penetration, Ijarah and housing.","keyPoints":["Insurance density = premiums per capita; penetration = premiums/GDP.","Insurance density = premiums per capita; penetration = premiums as % of GDP (p. 428)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Density and penetration","kind":"textbook","text":"Low premiums per person (density) and low premiums relative to GDP (penetration) in Muslim countries reflect a belief that insurance is un-Islamic. Growing Ijarah and home finance are now creating demand for Takaful (p. 428)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t16.7","t18.3.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"The first Takaful company was established in:","options":["Malaysia, 1984","Sudan, 1979","Saudi Arabia, 1990","Bahrain, 1975"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 428."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch16-t8"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch16-t8","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"16.6","sectionTitle":"Status and Potential of the Takaful Industry","title":"Status and Potential of the Takaful Industry","covers":["t16.6"],"overview":"Takaful has grown rapidly since 1979 but still lags Islamic banking's success at a global level, with huge untapped potential due to low insurance penetration in Muslim-majority markets.","simpleExplanation":"Takaful started in Sudan in 1979 and has since spread to over 60 companies in 24 countries, growing much faster than conventional insurance globally - but it still hasn't caught up to how successful Islamic banking has become, mainly because building a Takaful company is expensive and insurance penetration among Muslims has traditionally been very low.","academicExplanation":"The first Takaful company, the Islamic Insurance Company of Sudan, was established in 1979; Malaysia started Takaful business in 1984, with the system gathering momentum in Saudi Arabia and other Middle Eastern countries. Takaful has grown at 10-20% p.a., compared to the global insurance industry's average growth of 5% p.a. There are over 60 companies offering Takaful services (including windows, ~5%) across 24 countries: Bahrain, Bangladesh, Brunei, Egypt, Ghana, Indonesia, Iran, Jordan, Kuwait, Luxembourg, Malaysia, Pakistan, Qatar, Saudi Arabia, Senegal, Singapore, Sri Lanka, Sudan, Trinidad & Tobago, Tunisia, Turkey, UAE and Yemen. Takaful products meet individual and corporate short- and long-term needs; re-Takaful business has developed in Malaysia, Bahrain, Saudi Arabia and UAE. At the global level, Takaful has not achieved a comparable degree of success to Islamic banking, for two reasons: (1) the huge investment required to compete with the conventional insurance industry; (2) regulatory changes required (as seen in Malaysia) to let Takaful compete on equal terms with conventional insurers. Takaful has huge potential given increasing demand for Shari'ah-compliant products, particularly alongside Islamic banking's growth; Islamic countries have shown low insurance density (premiums per capita) and low penetration (premiums as % of GDP), mainly because most Muslims believe insurance is un-Islamic and no alternative was available. Growth in Ijarah and home mortgages under Islamic banking has increased the need for Takaful cover, as has demand for personal policies (motor vehicles, health, family security). Realizing this potential requires public education/awareness, especially about family (life) Takaful, where only a small number of companies offer comprehensive policies - in the Middle East, only Bank Aljazira (Saudi Arabia) offers exclusive Takaful Ta'awani (family Takaful) with a full product range (retirement, marriage, education, protection); other companies mainly run composite operations. Islamic banks may realize this potential by jointly establishing well-capitalized Takaful companies where a viable facility doesn't yet exist (legal constraints permitting), primarily to serve Islamic banks' Shari'ah-compliant Takaful needs (though they may also do other market business), creating competition and more general Takaful companies with competitive pricing entering the market.","examExplanation":"Timeline: first Takaful company 1979 (Sudan), Malaysia 1984. Growth rate: 10-20% p.a. vs global insurance industry's 5% p.a. Footprint: 60+ companies, 24 countries. Two reasons Takaful lags Islamic banking globally: (1) huge capital investment needed to compete with conventional insurers; (2) regulatory changes needed for equal competitive footing.","keyPoints":["The first Takaful company, the Islamic Insurance Company of Sudan, was established in 1979; Malaysia followed in 1984","Takaful has grown at 10-20% p.a., roughly two to four times the global insurance industry's average growth of 5% p.a.","Over 60 companies offer Takaful services (including windows) across 24 countries","Takaful has not matched Islamic banking's global success, mainly due to the huge investment needed to compete with conventional insurers and required regulatory changes","Low insurance density and penetration in Muslim-majority countries reflect huge untapped Takaful potential, driven partly by belief that insurance is un-Islamic","Bank Aljazira (Saudi Arabia) is cited as the only Middle Eastern company offering exclusive, comprehensive family Takaful (Takaful Ta'awani) with a full product range"],"definitions":[{"term":"Insurance Density","definition":"Insurance premiums per capita in a given market, used as a measure of insurance market development; noted to be low in most Islamic countries."},{"term":"Insurance Penetration","definition":"Insurance premiums as a percentage of GDP, another standard measure of insurance market development; also noted to be low in Islamic countries."}],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Bank Aljazira's Takaful Ta'awani","kind":"textbook","text":"In the Middle East, only Bank Aljazira of Saudi Arabia offers exclusive Takaful Ta'awani (family Takaful) with a full range of products - retirement, marriage, education and protection - for both general public and corporate clients; other companies mainly engage in composite operations."}],"commonConfusions":["Assuming Takaful has grown as successfully worldwide as Islamic banking - the text is explicit that it has not, for identifiable structural and regulatory reasons"],"distinctions":["Insurance density (premiums per capita) versus insurance penetration (premiums as % of GDP) - both cited as low in Islamic countries"],"relatedConcepts":["Re-Takaful","Family Takaful","General Takaful","Islamic banking"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.6","pages":[428,429]}}]},{"id":"t16.7","section":"16.7","title":"Takaful Challenges","pages":[429,430],"tier":"core","concepts":["takaful"],"intuition":"What must change for Takaful to reach its potential?","simpleExplanation":"Challenges: incorporating Waqf; adequate capitalisation (family Takaful absorbs cash in early years); human resources and training; re-Takaful availability (few providers, mostly Mudarabah-based, which is objectionable); compliant investment avenues; standardisation and harmonisation (AAOIFI, IFSB) with convergence on the Wakalah–Waqf model; legal and regulatory frameworks; competitiveness; and above all public awareness. Every company needs a Shari’ah board/advisor and periodic Shari’ah audit.","academicExplanation":["Composite (general) business offers early cash flow; family Takaful's real potential needs a strong capital base. Regulators should allow flexibility while applying benchmarks such as CAMELS.","The author says the model should converge on the Waqf, or Wakalah–Waqf, structure, which fully suits Tabarru‘ and is free of Shari’ah objections (pp. 429–430). Beyond this, the challenges are adequate capital, human resources, re-Takaful capacity (limited, and mostly on a Mudarabah basis, which is objectionable), compliant investment avenues, standardisation, and legal and regulatory frameworks (pp. 429–430). The biggest challenge is awareness: Muslims who avoided insurance on religious grounds must be reassured. Every Takaful company needs a Shari’ah board or advisor and periodic Shari’ah audit (p. 429). Family Takaful (Takaful Ta‘awani) holds the real potential but absorbs cash in early years, so a stronger capital base is needed. Composite annual business provides early cash flow. Training bodies such as IRTI, AAOIFI, central banks and SECs should help. AAOIFI and IFSB should set standards, and regulators should apply benchmarks such as CAMELS (pp. 429–430)."],"examExplanation":"Challenges: awareness (biggest), Waqf adoption, capital, HR/training, re-Takaful, investments, standardisation (Wakalah–Waqf convergence), regulation, competition, Shari’ah governance.","keyPoints":["Most important challenge: awareness."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Why family Takaful needs capital","kind":"textbook","text":"A new company’s annual general policies bring early cash, while its family Takaful business absorbs cash in its first years. Without a strong capital base it cannot grow the family line, where the real potential lies (p. 429)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Family Takaful generates early cash flow for a new company.","right":"Family programmes absorb cash in the early years; composite annual products provide the early cash flow (p. 429)."}],"relatedTopics":["t16.4.1","t18.3.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"What does the chapter call the most important challenge for Takaful?","options":["Tax rates","Creating awareness of the concept","Too many companies","Excess capital"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 429."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch16-t9"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch16-t9","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"16.7","sectionTitle":"Takaful Challenges","title":"Takaful Challenges","covers":["t16.7"],"overview":"Takaful faces conceptual, capitalization, human resource, re-Takaful availability and standardization challenges that must be resolved to build long-term credibility and growth.","simpleExplanation":"For Takaful to keep growing, the industry needs to solve several real problems at once: convincing the public it's genuinely different from conventional insurance, raising enough capital to compete, training qualified staff, finding enough re-Takaful (reinsurance) providers, and getting scholars to agree on standardized models - especially converging on the Wakalah-Waqf approach that's closest to Shari'ah principles.","academicExplanation":"The basic structure of a Shari'ah-compliant insurance system (mutual help among group members) is generally accepted by scholars; the way ahead is to improve and develop existing operational structures and procedures. Takaful operators should incorporate the Waqf institution into their arrangements, since it fully conforms to Tabarru' requirements and is free of Shari'ah-related objections; other problems needing attention include adequate capitalization (to compete with conventional companies), human resource development, re-Takaful facilities, Shari'ah-compliant investment avenues, standardization/harmonization of practices, and legal/regulatory frameworks for healthy industry operation. The single most important challenge is creating awareness about the Takaful concept itself, since the majority of Muslims avoiding insurance for religious reasons need assurance that Shari'ah-compliant cover involves no prohibitions. Every Takaful company requires a Shari'ah board/advisor and periodical Shari'ah audit, both to ensure compliance and to build public confidence. Most Takaful companies currently provide general business policies; the real growth potential lies in family Takaful (Takaful Ta'awani), which requires mobilizing the general public and a sound financial base - composite business benefits from expense-and-surplus-driven annual products giving early cash flow, whereas family Takaful programmes are cash-absorbing in a company's early years, a problem solvable only by enhancing capital bases. Working competitively alongside conventional insurers is itself a challenge, requiring competitive pricing, quality coverage, delivery time, a new professional mindset avoiding malpractice, and proactive policyholder support to keep companies competitive and Shari'ah-compliant. Re-Takaful availability is a problem, with only a few companies providing it (in Malaysia, UAE, Bahrain and Saudi Arabia), and even then mostly on a Mudarabah basis - itself Shari'ah-objectionable per the chapter's earlier discussion. Well-trained manpower is another major challenge, given that Islamic banking training facilities exist while Takaful training facilities are almost entirely lacking, requiring proactive collaboration between the IRTI (IDB), the AAOIFI, central bank training arms and Securities and Exchange Commissions with Takaful companies. Finally, given financial services globalization, product standardization is essential, requiring the AAOIFI and IFSB to prepare Shari'ah and performance standards respectively (as done for Islamic banking); differences of opinion on Takaful models must be resolved, since unresolved reservations agitate stakeholders' minds and hinder industry growth - the chapter recommends convergence on the Wakalah-Waqf model as the basis nearest to Shari'ah principles, with regulators providing flexibility while ensuring operators meet accepted benchmarks like CAMELS tests and other performance criteria. An appendix lists numerous historical Fatawa on insurance from 1900 to 1995 across multiple countries, illustrating both opposition (e.g. Shaikh al-Azhar's 1995 Fatwah against life insurance, Egypt's 1926 Supreme Court verdict, several Malaysian state Fatawa against conventional insurance) and support (e.g. Shaikh Mohammad Abduh's 1900-1901 Fatawa favouring insurance's validity, the Higher Council of Saudi Ulama and the Fiqh Council of the Muslim World League favouring insurance under an Islamic model, and the OIC's Fiqh Council favouring insurance under an Islamic model) - reflecting the century-long scholarly discussion that eventually converged on Takaful as the accepted Shari'ah-compliant alternative.","examExplanation":"Key challenges: (1) public awareness/education (the single most important); (2) mandatory Shari'ah board/advisor + periodic audit per company; (3) capital base for family Takaful (cash-absorbing early on, unlike composite business); (4) competitiveness vs conventional insurers; (5) re-Takaful scarcity, and what exists is mostly Mudarabah-based (still Shari'ah-objectionable); (6) human resource/training scarcity (unlike Islamic banking); (7) standardization via AAOIFI/IFSB, converging toward the Wakalah-Waqf model.","keyPoints":["The single most important Takaful industry challenge identified is creating public awareness that Shari'ah-compliant cover involves no prohibitions","Every Takaful company needs a Shari'ah board/advisor and periodical Shari'ah audit, both for compliance and public confidence","Family Takaful is cash-absorbing in a company's early years (unlike composite business), requiring an enhanced capital base to sustain it","Re-Takaful is scarce, available in only a few countries, and even then mostly structured on the Shari'ah-objectionable Mudarabah basis","Trained human resources for Takaful are almost entirely lacking, in contrast to the more developed training infrastructure for Islamic banking","The chapter recommends industry convergence on the Wakalah-Waqf model, as the basis nearest to Shari'ah principles, alongside AAOIFI/IFSB-led standardization"],"definitions":[],"conditions":["Every Takaful company requires a Shari'ah board or advisor and periodical Shari'ah audit"],"principles":["Takaful operators should incorporate the Waqf institution into their arrangements, since it fully conforms to Tabarru' requirements and is free of Shari'ah-related objections"],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Historical Fatawa on Insurance (Appendix)","kind":"textbook","text":"Fatawa on insurance span nearly a century and diverge sharply: Shaikh Mohammad Abduh's 1900-1901 Fatawa favoured insurance's validity, while a 1965 unanimous Muslim League Conference Fatwah in Cairo opposed life insurance; later, the Higher Council of Saudi Ulama, the Fiqh Council of the Muslim World League and the OIC's Fiqh Council all favoured insurance under an Islamic (Takaful) model, reflecting the scholarly convergence the chapter describes."}],"commonConfusions":["Assuming re-Takaful is widely available on a fully Shari'ah-uncontroversial basis - the chapter notes it is scarce and mostly Mudarabah-based, which itself carries the Shari'ah objections discussed earlier in the chapter"],"distinctions":["Composite Takaful business (early cash flow, expense/surplus-driven annual products) versus family Takaful (cash-absorbing in early years, requiring a stronger capital base)"],"relatedConcepts":["Wakalah-Waqf model","Re-Takaful","AAOIFI","IFSB","CAMELS"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.7","pages":[429,430,431]}}]},{"id":"t16.appx","section":"Appendix","title":"Appendix: Fatawa on Insurance","pages":[430,431],"tier":"revision","concepts":["takaful","fatwa"],"intuition":"How have juristic bodies ruled on insurance over the last century?","simpleExplanation":"The appendix lists Fatawa: against life/conventional insurance — Shaikh al-Azhar (1995), Makkah judicial conference (1398 AH), Morocco seminar (1972), Egypt Supreme Court (1926), Malaysia National Religious Council (1972), Cairo Muslim League conference (1965), and several Malaysian states; in favour — Mohammad Abduh (1900–01) and Mohd Baqit (1906); in favour of an Islamic model — Saudi Higher Council of Ulama (1397 AH), Muslim World League Fiqh Council (1398 AH), OIC Fiqh Council (1405 AH) and the First International Conference on Islamic Economy (1396 AH).","academicExplanation":["The pattern shows opposition to conventional insurance and acceptance of an Islamic cooperative model."],"examExplanation":"Fatawa: majority against conventional insurance; OIC Fiqh Council (1405 AH), Saudi Ulama and MWL favour Islamic models.","keyPoints":["Abduh's early Fatawa favoured insurance.","Early Egyptian muftis (Abduh, Baqit) favoured insurance. Later councils rejected conventional insurance but approved an Islamic cooperative model (pp. 430–431)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Changing positions over time","kind":"textbook","text":"Early Egyptian muftis Abduh (1900–1901) and Baqit (1906) favoured insurance. Later councils rejected conventional insurance but approved an Islamic model — the Higher Council of Saudi Ulama (1397 AH), the MWL Fiqh Council (1398 AH) and the OIC Fiqh Council (1405 AH) (pp. 430–431)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t16.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which body's Fatwa (1405 AH) favoured insurance under an Islamic model?","options":["Egypt Supreme Court","OIC Fiqh Council","Makkah judicial conference","Selangor State"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 431."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"table":{"caption":"Selected Fatawa on insurance (pp. 430–431)","head":["Source","Position"],"rows":[["Shaikh Mohammad Abduh, 1900–1901","In favour of insurance"],["Shaikh Mohd Baqit, 1906","In favour of insurance"],["Supreme Court of Egypt, 1926","Against life insurance"],["Muslim League Conference, Cairo, 1965","Against life insurance"],["Morocco seminar, 1972","Against life insurance"],["National Religious Council (Malaysia), 1972","Against conventional insurance"],["First International Conference on Islamic Economy, Makkah, 1396 AH","In favour of an Islamic model"],["Higher Council of Saudi Ulama, 1397 AH","In favour of an Islamic model"],["Fiqh Council of the Muslim World League, 1398 AH","In favour of an Islamic model"],["OIC Fiqh Council, 1405 AH","In favour of an Islamic model"],["Shaikh al-Azhar Jad al-Haq, 1995","Against life insurance"]]}}],"questions":[{"id":"q16.1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which is NOT cited as a reason conventional insurance is prohibited?","options":["Riba","Gharar","Maisir/Qimar","Payment of claims to victims"],"correctAnswer":3,"explanation":"p. 419.","topicId":"t16.2.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall prohibitions","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","pages":[418,420]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.2","type":"tf","prompt":"In the Takaful system, the underwriting surplus belongs to the Takaful operator's shareholders.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"It belongs to participants (pp. 421, 423).","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"section":"16.3","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply surplus rule","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420,421]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.3","type":"scenario","prompt":"A Takaful operator takes 50% of the underwriting surplus in good years but bears nothing when claims exceed contributions. Which model and objection apply?","options":["Waqf model — no objection","Mudarabah model — operator takes UWS but not UWL, becoming a risk-taker","Wakalah — fee too low","Conventional mutual — acceptable"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 424, 426.","topicId":"t16.4.2","chapter":16,"section":"16.4.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Evaluate models","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.2","pages":[426,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.4","type":"multi","prompt":"Which are foundations of Takaful described in the chapter? (Select all.)","options":["‘Aqilah","Tabarru‘","Waqf","Bai‘ al ‘Inah","Mutual help funds of early traders"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,4],"explanation":"pp. 420–422.","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"section":"16.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall bases","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420,421]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.5","type":"application","prompt":"Box 16.1 variant: participants donate Rs.10m; operator fee 30%; claims and re-Takaful Rs.5m paid from the fund. What is the underwriting surplus?","options":["Rs.5m","Rs.2m","Rs.7m","Rs.3m"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Fund after fee = 7m; less claims 5m = Rs.2m surplus (belonging to participants). (Practice example — generated for learning.)","topicId":"t16.4.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Compute UWS","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","pages":[423,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.6","type":"match","prompt":"Match each Takaful model to its operator income.","pairs":[["Wakalah","Pre-agreed fee (% of contributions)"],["Mudarabah","Share of profit including surplus"],["Waqf","Management fee + Mudarib share of investment profit"],["Wakalah–Mudarabah","Fee plus share of surplus and investment profit"]],"explanation":"Section 16.4.1.","topicId":"t16.4.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Match models","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","pages":[423,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.7","type":"comparison","prompt":"Which statement correctly contrasts family Takaful with conventional life insurance?","options":["Both pay a nominee regardless of inheritance law","Family Takaful is based on Waqf, Tabarru‘ and Mudarabah, with benefits to heirs per inheritance; conventional life is Riba-based with a nominee","Conventional agents are paid by the company","Family Takaful guarantees interest"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 427.","topicId":"t16.5","chapter":16,"section":"16.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Compare life cover","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.5","pages":[427,428]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.8","type":"identify","prompt":"A model in which shareholders make an initial donation to create a fund whose surplus/deficit belongs to the fund is:","options":["Mudarabah model","Waqf model","Wakalah model","Conventional stock insurer"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 424.","topicId":"t16.4.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify Waqf model","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","pages":[423,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.9","type":"short","prompt":"Refute the argument that insurance cover contradicts trust in Allah.","keywords":["camel","ease","myth","Riba","Gharar"],"correctAnswer":"The author treats it as a myth: Islam directs people to avoid harm where possible and lighten their burdens; Shari’ah intends ease (Qur’an 2:185, 4:28); the Prophet (pbuh) said ‘Tie your camel first, then put your trust in Allah.’ The real objection is to Riba, Gharar and gambling in conventional contracts, which Takaful removes.","explanation":"pp. 417–418.","topicId":"t16.2","chapter":16,"section":"16.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Argue from sources","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2","pages":[417,418]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.10","type":"order","prompt":"Order how Takaful deficits are absorbed (Section 16.5).","items":["Reserves","Interest-free loan (Qard al Hasan) from shareholders","General increase in pricing"],"explanation":"p. 427.","topicId":"t16.5","chapter":16,"section":"16.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Sequence deficit absorption","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.5","pages":[427,428]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.11","type":"definition","prompt":"Tabarru‘ in Takaful means:","options":["Interest on contributions","A donation relinquished to help fellow participants","A loan to the operator","The operator's fee"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 421.","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"section":"16.3","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define Tabarru‘","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420,421]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.12","type":"tf","prompt":"For a large corporate client, a Takaful operator should reduce the risk premium rather than its own fee.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"It should reduce the operator's fee; risk premium should be uniform for similar risks (p. 426).","topicId":"t16.4.3","chapter":16,"section":"16.4.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply pricing rule","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.3","pages":[426,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.13","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which two reasons does the author give for Muslim societies avoiding insurance?","options":["Cost and lack of companies","A belief that it is unnecessary given mutual help and faith in destiny, and the prohibition of Riba, Gharar and gambling","Government bans and taxes","Language and culture"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"He calls the first a myth and the second genuine (pp. 417–418).","topicId":"t16.2","chapter":16,"section":"16.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall reasons for avoiding insurance","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2","pages":[417,418]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.14","type":"identify","prompt":"Which Hadith is cited to show that precaution is compatible with Tawakkul?","options":["“Religion is sincere advice”","“Tie your camel first, then put your trust in Allah”","“Seek knowledge”","“Actions are by intentions”"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 418.","topicId":"t16.2","chapter":16,"section":"16.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the basis for precaution","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2","pages":[417,418]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.15","type":"tf","prompt":"Islamic insurance began at about the same time as Islamic banking in the early 1960s.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Islamic banking emerged in the 1960s–70s; Islamic insurance began no earlier than 1979 (p. 418).","topicId":"t16.2","chapter":16,"section":"16.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the history of Takaful","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2","pages":[417,418]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.16","type":"identify","prompt":"Who was the first jurist to discuss modern commercial (marine) insurance in detail?","options":["Imam Abu Hanifa","Ibn Abdin (19th century)","Ibn Taymiyah","Shaikh Abu Zahra"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"He did not approve it (pp. 418–419).","topicId":"t16.2.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall early juristic views on insurance","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","pages":[418,420]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.17","type":"match","prompt":"Match each prohibited element to how it arises in conventional insurance.","pairs":[["Riba (direct)","Excess between premiums and sum insured"],["Riba (indirect)","Investment of funds in interest-based business"],["Qimar","One party’s gain depends on the other’s loss"],["Khatar","Rights and liabilities remain contingent"]],"explanation":"pp. 419–420.","topicId":"t16.2.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Analyse the prohibited elements","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","pages":[418,420]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.18","type":"short","prompt":"Why did scholars once differ on the status of insurance?","keywords":["detail","text","direct"],"correctAnswer":"Jurists were asked for rulings without enough detail about insurance, and there is no direct text on it in the Qur’an and Sunnah.","explanation":"p. 419.","topicId":"t16.2.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the differences of opinion","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","pages":[418,420]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.19","type":"tf","prompt":"Gharar invalidates conventional insurance because insurance is a commutative contract.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"Gharar is prohibited in commercial or commutative contracts (p. 420).","topicId":"t16.2.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply the Gharar rule to insurance","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","pages":[418,420]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.20","type":"definition","prompt":"Ta’mein refers to:","options":["Cooperative mutual aid","Reassuring, safeguarding and guaranteeing through indemnity; implying guarantee more than cooperative sharing","Charity","Endowment"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 420.","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"section":"16.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define Ta’mein","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420,421]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.21","type":"identify","prompt":"When did the OIC Islamic Fiqh Council approve cooperative Takaful?","options":["1972","1979","1985","2000"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 420.","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"section":"16.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the OIC approval of Takaful","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420,421]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.22","type":"match","prompt":"Match each early practice to its description.","pairs":[["‘Aqilah","Kinsmen sharing blood money and calamities"],["Qasamah","Oath taken from the kinsmen of the murdered"],["Mawalat","Bequest in return for paying blood money"],["Traders’ fund","Pool for sea-voyage mishaps and robberies"]],"explanation":"pp. 420–421.","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"section":"16.3","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall historical precedents of Takaful","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420,421]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.23","type":"multi","prompt":"Which statements about Waqf in Takaful are correct? (Select all.)","options":["Waqf is a separate entity able to own property","Waqf property cannot be sold; only its usufruct is assigned","The donor may be one of the beneficiaries","Waqf property returns to donors on request"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"p. 421.","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"section":"16.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Understand Waqf in Takaful","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420,421]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.24","type":"tf","prompt":"The operator’s return in Takaful is the underwriting surplus.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"The UWS/UWL belongs to the group; the operator earns a fee and/or share of investment profit (p. 421).","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"section":"16.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish UWS from investment profit","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420,421]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.25","type":"identify","prompt":"What is the main objective of Takaful for policyholders?","options":["Windfall gains","Mutual help","Tax savings","Speculation"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 422.","topicId":"t16.3.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.3.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the objective of Takaful","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3.1","pages":[422,422]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.26","type":"comparison","prompt":"Under the Wakalah basis, how is a family Takaful contribution divided?","options":["Two parts: protection and investment","Three parts: management fee, protection and investment","One part: donation","Four parts including interest"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Under Mudarabah, two parts (p. 422).","topicId":"t16.4","chapter":16,"section":"16.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Compare contribution splits","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4","pages":[422,423]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.27","type":"scenario","prompt":"Claims in a general Takaful fund exceed the fund and its reserves. What does the company provide?","options":["A grant it keeps as profit","Qard al Hasan to the fund","An interest-bearing loan","Nothing; claims are cut"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 423.","topicId":"t16.4","chapter":16,"section":"16.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply deficit rules","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4","pages":[422,423]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.28","type":"tf","prompt":"According to the chapter, every donation is Sadaqah, so a Takaful donor can never benefit from the fund.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Not every donation is Sadaqah; a Waqf donor may benefit as a beneficiary (p. 423).","topicId":"t16.4","chapter":16,"section":"16.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish Tabarru‘ from Sadaqah","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4","pages":[422,423]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.29","type":"identify","prompt":"Where is the pure Wakalah model mainly practised?","options":["Asia–Pacific","Middle East","Europe","Africa only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Mudarabah mainly in the Asia–Pacific (p. 424).","topicId":"t16.4.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall where the models are practised","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","pages":[423,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.30","type":"application","prompt":"Box 16.1: participants donate Rs.20 m. The operator fee is 25 %, claims and re-Takaful cost Rs.9 m and investment profit is Rs.2 m, shared fund 60 : company 40. What is the underwriting surplus?","options":["Rs.6 m","Rs.11 m","Rs.15 m","Rs.5 m"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"20 − 5 (fee) − 9 = 6 m UWS for participants. Separately, the fund gets 1.2 m and the company 0.8 m of the investment profit (p. 425).","topicId":"t16.4.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Calculate Wakalah–Waqf results","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","pages":[423,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.31","type":"order","prompt":"Order the Wakalah–Waqf process in Box 16.1.","items":["Shareholders create the Waqf fund with an initial donation","Participants donate to the Waqf fund","The company takes its 25–30 % management fee and invests the fund","Claims and re-Takaful costs are charged to the fund; UWS/UWL belongs to participants","Investment profit is shared between fund and company by Mudarabah"],"explanation":"p. 425.","topicId":"t16.4.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Sequence the Wakalah–Waqf model","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","pages":[423,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.32","type":"tf","prompt":"In the Waqf model, the fund is obliged to distribute all underwriting surplus to participants each year.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"There is no obligation; the surplus may be distributed, reserved or reinvested (p. 424).","topicId":"t16.4.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply Waqf surplus rules","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","pages":[423,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.33","type":"multi","prompt":"Which objections are raised against the Mudarabah Takaful model? (Select all.)","options":["A donation cannot simultaneously be Mudarabah capital","The operator takes UWS but bears no UWL","Non-life contributions are not returned as Mudarabah capital must be","A Mudarib giving Qard al Hasan acts as a guarantor"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,3],"explanation":"p. 426.","topicId":"t16.4.2","chapter":16,"section":"16.4.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Analyse the Mudarabah model","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.2","pages":[426,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.34","type":"scenario","prompt":"A Takaful operator offers a large corporate client a lower rate. According to the chapter, what should it reduce?","options":["The risk premium","The operator’s fee, not the risk premium","Claims paid","Reserves"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 426.","topicId":"t16.4.3","chapter":16,"section":"16.4.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply pricing principles","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.3","pages":[426,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.35","type":"identify","prompt":"Who should bear the initial set-up expenses of a Takaful company?","options":["Participants","Shareholders","The Waqf fund","Re-Takaful operators"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 426.","topicId":"t16.4.3","chapter":16,"section":"16.4.3","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall expense allocation","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.3","pages":[426,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.36","type":"comparison","prompt":"How are agents paid in conventional life insurance and in family Takaful?","options":["Both from premiums","Conventional: from assureds’ premiums; Takaful: by the company itself","Both by the company","Takaful agents are unpaid"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 427.","topicId":"t16.5","chapter":16,"section":"16.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Compare life policies","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.5","pages":[427,428]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.37","type":"scenario","prompt":"A family Takaful participant surrenders after four years. What does he receive?","options":["The full sum covered","His investment part with profit and a reduced pro rata UWS share, but no help from the Takaful fund","Nothing","Premiums plus interest"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 428.","topicId":"t16.5","chapter":16,"section":"16.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply early-termination rules","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.5","pages":[427,428]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.38","type":"identify","prompt":"On the insured’s death, to whom does the insurable interest vest under the Islamic model?","options":["A nominee chosen freely","The assured or his heirs according to inheritance and wills","The company","The Waqf"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 427.","topicId":"t16.5","chapter":16,"section":"16.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall insurable interest rules","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.5","pages":[427,428]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.39","type":"mcq","prompt":"The first Takaful company was established in:","options":["Malaysia, 1984","Sudan, 1979","Saudi Arabia, 1985","Egypt, 1926"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The Islamic Insurance Company of Sudan (p. 428).","topicId":"t16.6","chapter":16,"section":"16.6","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall Takaful milestones","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.6","pages":[428,429]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.40","type":"multi","prompt":"Why has Takaful not matched the success of Islamic banking globally? (Select all.)","options":["Huge investment needed to compete with conventional insurers","Regulatory changes needed to compete on equal terms","Takaful is prohibited by most scholars","No demand exists"],"correctAnswer":[0,1],"explanation":"p. 428.","topicId":"t16.6","chapter":16,"section":"16.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall constraints on Takaful growth","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.6","pages":[428,429]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.41","type":"definition","prompt":"Insurance penetration means:","options":["Premiums per capita","Premiums as a percentage of GDP","Number of companies","Claims ratio"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Density is premiums per capita (p. 428).","topicId":"t16.6","chapter":16,"section":"16.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define insurance indicators","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.6","pages":[428,429]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.42","type":"identify","prompt":"What does the chapter call the most important challenge for Takaful?","options":["IT systems","Creating awareness of the concept among Muslims who avoided insurance","Lack of Shari’ah boards","Tax"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 429.","topicId":"t16.7","chapter":16,"section":"16.7","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall Takaful challenges","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.7","pages":[429,430]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.43","type":"tf","prompt":"Family Takaful programmes provide early cash flow for new Takaful companies.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"They absorb cash in early years; composite annual products provide cash flow (p. 429).","topicId":"t16.7","chapter":16,"section":"16.7","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Understand family Takaful economics","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.7","pages":[429,430]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q16.44","type":"identify","prompt":"Which early mufti issued fatawa in favour of insurance in 1900–1901?","options":["Shaikh Jad al-Haq","Shaikh Mohammad Abduh","Ibn Abdin","Shaikh Abu Zahra"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Appendix (p. 430).","topicId":"t16.appx","chapter":16,"section":"Appendix","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall fatawa on insurance","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"Appendix","pages":[430,431]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q-ch16-1","type":"mcq","prompt":"The chapter describes one common reason Muslims historically avoided insurance as 'only a myth'. What is that reason?","explanation":"The chapter states this belief is only a myth, illustrated by the Hadith of the Prophet telling a man to tie his camel first, then trust in Allah - showing precaution-taking is not against Islamic belief.","topicId":"t16.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.1-16.2","topicLabel":"The Need for Takaful Cover","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain why conventional insurance is considered non-compliant with Shari'ah","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2","pages":[417,418]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The belief that insurance always involves Riba","The belief that trusting Allah/destiny makes seeking any risk cover unnecessary","The belief that insurance companies are always foreign-owned","The belief that insurance is prohibited by explicit Qur'anic text"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch16-2","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: Takaful emerged before Islamic banking, in the 1960s.","explanation":"False - Islamic banking emerged in the 1960s-70s, while Islamic insurance (Takaful) started no earlier than 1979, roughly a decade later.","topicId":"t16.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.1-16.2","topicLabel":"The Need for Takaful Cover","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain why conventional insurance is considered non-compliant with Shari'ah","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2","pages":[418]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch16-3","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are cited as Shari'ah objections to conventional insurance?","explanation":"The chapter cites Riba, Qimar/Maisir and Gharar (plus invalid risk transfer) as the core objections - documentation itself is not a Shari'ah objection (indeed, the Qur'an encourages documentation of transactions).","topicId":"t16.2.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","topicLabel":"Why Conventional Insurance is Prohibited","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain why conventional insurance is considered non-compliant with Shari'ah","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","pages":[419]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Riba (interest)","Qimar/Maisir (gambling)","Gharar (excessive uncertainty)","Excessive use of written documentation"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2]},{"id":"q-ch16-4","type":"short","prompt":"Explain why the presence of Gharar (uncertainty) specifically invalidates conventional insurance, according to the chapter's reasoning.","explanation":"The chapter is explicit that Gharar is prohibited when involved in commutative contracts, distinguishing conventional insurance's structure from purely donative arrangements where uncertainty is more tolerated.","topicId":"t16.2.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","topicLabel":"Why Conventional Insurance is Prohibited","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain why conventional insurance is considered non-compliant with Shari'ah","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","pages":[419,420]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Because conventional insurance is a commutative (exchange) contract, and Gharar/uncertainty is prohibited specifically when involved in commercial/commutative contracts - the policyholder's liability and right both remain contingent, and premiums are lost entirely if the insured event does not occur, making it resemble a money stake or gamble."},{"id":"q-ch16-5","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which historical Arab institution do contemporary jurists identify as the foundational principle underlying Takaful?","explanation":"Contemporary jurists acknowledge that the principle of shared responsibility in the system of 'Aqilah (kinsmen), approved by the Prophet, laid the foundation for Takaful.","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"section":"16.3","topicLabel":"The Shari'ah Basis of Takaful","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe the Shari'ah basis of Takaful, including Tabarru' and Waqf","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420,421]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Bai' al 'Inah","'Aqilah (kinsmen sharing financial responsibility)","Tawarruq","Bai' al Dayn"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch16-6","type":"comparison","prompt":"How does a participant's benefit from a Takaful Waqf fund differ from an ordinary Sadaqah (charity) donation?","explanation":"The chapter explains that like Sadaqah, the donor to a Waqf relinquishes individual ownership; but in contrast to Sadaqah, the donor can benefit from the fund as one of its beneficiaries - a key reason the Waqf-based Takaful model is considered preferable.","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"section":"16.3","topicLabel":"The Shari'ah Basis of Takaful","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Describe the Shari'ah basis of Takaful, including Tabarru' and Waqf","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[421]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["They are identical - once given, neither can be reclaimed or benefited from","Like Sadaqah, the donor relinquishes individual ownership; but unlike Sadaqah, the donor can still benefit as one of the beneficiaries of the Waqf fund","Waqf donations must always be repaid with interest","Sadaqah donors retain full ownership while Waqf donors do not"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch16-7","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main objective of the Takaful system from the policyholders' point of view?","explanation":"The chapter states the main objective of the Takaful system from the policyholders' point of view is mutual help and not earning profit or windfall gains, unlike conventional insurance.","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"section":"16.3","topicLabel":"The Shari'ah Basis of Takaful","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Describe the Shari'ah basis of Takaful, including Tabarru' and Waqf","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3.1","pages":[422]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Earning maximum profit","Mutual help, not profit or windfall gain","Avoiding all documentation","Competing directly with conventional insurers on price alone"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch16-8","type":"order","prompt":"Order the steps of how the Takaful system works, from contribution to surplus distribution.","explanation":"The correct order is: participant pays contribution (split into protection/investment parts) -> protection part donated as Tabarru' to the fund -> claims paid from the fund for defined losses -> underwriting surplus distributed to or retained for participants.","topicId":"t16.4","chapter":16,"section":"16.4","topicLabel":"How the Takaful System Works","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain how the Takaful system works, including the treatment of underwriting surplus/deficit","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4","pages":[422,423]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"items":["Participant pays contribution, split into protection and investment parts","Protection part donated to the Takaful fund as Tabarru'","Claims paid from the Takaful fund for defined losses","Underwriting surplus distributed to/retained for participants as a group"]},{"id":"q-ch16-9","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does the Takaful operator do if claims at any time exceed the amount available in the fund and reserves?","explanation":"There is a provision of Qard al Hasan by the company to the fund if claims at any time exceed the amount available in it and the reserves are insufficient to meet the shortfall.","topicId":"t16.4","chapter":16,"section":"16.4","topicLabel":"How the Takaful System Works","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain how the Takaful system works, including the treatment of underwriting surplus/deficit","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4","pages":[423]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The operator simply refuses to pay further claims","The operator provides Qard al Hasan (an interest-free loan) to the fund","The operator raises all participants' contributions immediately without notice","The operator dissolves the Takaful company"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch16-10","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: Per the settled scholarly consensus described in the chapter, the Takaful operator is entitled to a share of the underwriting surplus (UWS) as well as investment profit.","explanation":"False - the settled consensus is that the whole UWS/UWL belongs to participants/policyholders; the operator should get only a Takaful fee and/or a share of investment profit, not a share of underwriting surplus.","topicId":"t16.4","chapter":16,"section":"16.4","topicLabel":"How the Takaful System Works","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain how the Takaful system works, including the treatment of underwriting surplus/deficit","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4","pages":[423]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch16-11","type":"comparison","prompt":"Compare the pure Wakalah and pure Mudarabah models of Takaful.","explanation":"In the pure Wakalah model, the operator earns a fixed percentage fee while participants bear the whole UWS/UWL; in the pure Mudarabah model, profit (investment returns plus UWS) is shared per an agreed ratio, but the operator does not bear underwriting losses - both features scholars have criticized.","topicId":"t16.4.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","topicLabel":"Models of Takaful","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Compare the Wakalah, Mudarabah, Waqf and Wakalah-Waqf models of Takaful and their respective Shari'ah issues","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","pages":[423,424]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Wakalah: operator earns an agreed % fee, participants bear full UWS/UWL. Mudarabah: profit (investment + UWS) shared per agreed ratio, but operator doesn't bear UWL","Both models are functionally identical","Wakalah involves profit-and-loss sharing while Mudarabah involves only a fixed fee","Neither model has ever been practised in any region"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch16-12","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to research conducted under Shaikh Muhammad Taqi Usmani's guidance, which model is the best basis for a practical, Shari'ah-compliant Takaful system?","explanation":"Per research by over forty Shari'ah scholars under Shaikh Muhammad Taqi Usmani's guidance, a Waqf model or a combination of Wakalah and Waqf is the best basis for evolving a practical Takaful system.","topicId":"t16.4.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","topicLabel":"Models of Takaful","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Compare the Wakalah, Mudarabah, Waqf and Wakalah-Waqf models of Takaful and their respective Shari'ah issues","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","pages":[423]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Pure Wakalah model alone","Pure Mudarabah model alone","A Waqf model, or a combination of Wakalah and Waqf","A conventional reinsurance-style model"],"correctAnswer":2},{"id":"q-ch16-13","type":"short","prompt":"In the Box 16.1 Wakalah-with-Waqf model example, what percentage of the Waqf fund does the company invest on a Wakalah basis, and what does it retain as its management fee?","explanation":"This is the specific split given in the chapter's Box 16.1 flowchart of the Wakalah-with-Waqf model.","topicId":"t16.4.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","topicLabel":"Models of Takaful","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Compare the Wakalah, Mudarabah, Waqf and Wakalah-Waqf models of Takaful and their respective Shari'ah issues","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","pages":[425]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"The company invests 70% to 75% of the Waqf fund on a Wakalah basis, retaining 25-30% as its management fee to cover all management expenses."},{"id":"q-ch16-14","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are valid Shari'ah objections to the Mudarabah model of Takaful, per the chapter?","explanation":"The chapter raises exactly these three objections to the Mudarabah model - plus the issue that requiring Qard al Hasan on deficit contradicts Mudarabah's pure profit-sharing definition. It is explicitly NOT fully accepted without reservation.","topicId":"t16.4.2","chapter":16,"section":"16.4.2-16.4.3","topicLabel":"Shari'ah Issues in Takaful Models","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Compare the Wakalah, Mudarabah, Waqf and Wakalah-Waqf models of Takaful and their respective Shari'ah issues","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.2","pages":[426]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["A donation (Tabarru') cannot simultaneously be Mudarabah capital","The operator gets a share of UWS but does not bear UWL","Non-life Takaful premiums are typically not returned to participants, unlike Mudarabah capital","The Mudarabah model is fully accepted by all scholars without reservation"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2]},{"id":"q-ch16-15","type":"scenario","prompt":"A Takaful company operating on a Wakalah-with-Shirkah basis gives shareholders a part of any underwriting surplus in addition to the operating fee. What is the Shari'ah concern raised by the chapter?","explanation":"The chapter states Wakalah combined with Shirkah arrangements is subject to the same objections as the Mudarabah model - the problem arises specifically when the operator is given a part of UWS in addition to the operating fee as a performance incentive, when surplus-sharing should remain among the pool members.","topicId":"t16.4.2","chapter":16,"section":"16.4.2-16.4.3","topicLabel":"Shari'ah Issues in Takaful Models","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Compare the Wakalah, Mudarabah, Waqf and Wakalah-Waqf models of Takaful and their respective Shari'ah issues","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.3","pages":[426]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["There is no concern; this is standard practice with no objections","This faces the same objection as the Mudarabah model - the operator becomes an inappropriate risk-taker by sharing UWS without bearing UWL","This is only a concern if the company is publicly listed","This is a concern only in Malaysia, not elsewhere"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch16-16","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the order in which Takaful losses (underwriting or investment) are absorbed, per the chapter?","explanation":"Losses in terms of underwriting or on investment, if any, are first absorbed by reserves, then from interest-free loans from shareholders, and then by a general increase in pricing by the company.","topicId":"t16.5","chapter":16,"section":"16.5","topicLabel":"Takaful and Conventional Insurance Compared","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Compare Takaful and conventional insurance across objectives, structure and investment policy","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.5","pages":[427]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["General pricing increase first, then reserves, then shareholder loans","Reserves first, then interest-free shareholder loans, then a general increase in pricing","Only ever a general pricing increase, with no reserves or loans involved","Government bailout first, then reserves"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch16-17","type":"comparison","prompt":"How does insurable interest differ between conventional life insurance and the Islamic (Takaful) model?","explanation":"Under the conventional system, insurable interest vests in the policyholder if alive at expiry, or the nominee if the assured dies within the period; under the Islamic model, it vests in the assured himself or his heirs per inheritance and will principles.","topicId":"t16.5","chapter":16,"section":"16.5","topicLabel":"Takaful and Conventional Insurance Compared","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Compare Takaful and conventional insurance across objectives, structure and investment policy","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.5","pages":[427]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Conventional: vests in the policyholder if alive at expiry, or a nominee if the assured dies within the term. Islamic model: vests in the assured or their heirs per inheritance/will principles","Both systems treat insurable interest identically","The Islamic model always vests interest in the Takaful company itself","Conventional insurance never allows a nominee"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch16-18","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which company was the first Takaful company, and where was it established?","explanation":"The first Takaful company was the Islamic Insurance Company of Sudan, established in 1979; Malaysia started Takaful business later, in 1984.","topicId":"t16.6","chapter":16,"section":"16.6","topicLabel":"Status and Potential of the Takaful Industry","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Assess the status, potential and key challenges facing the global Takaful industry","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.6","pages":[428]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Bank Aljazira, Saudi Arabia","The Islamic Insurance Company of Sudan","Kuwait Finance House, Kuwait","Malaysia's first Takaful company in 1979"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch16-19","type":"short","prompt":"Give the two reasons the chapter provides for why Takaful has not matched Islamic banking's degree of global success.","explanation":"These are explicitly listed as the two reasons in section 16.6.","topicId":"t16.6","chapter":16,"section":"16.6","topicLabel":"Status and Potential of the Takaful Industry","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Assess the status, potential and key challenges facing the global Takaful industry","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.6","pages":[428]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"(1) The huge investment required to compete with the established conventional insurance industry; (2) the regulatory changes required (as seen in Malaysia) to allow Takaful to compete with conventional insurers on equal terms."},{"id":"q-ch16-20","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does the chapter identify as the single most important challenge facing the Takaful industry?","explanation":"The chapter states the most important challenge the Takaful industry faces is creating awareness about the concept itself, since the majority of Muslims avoiding insurance for religious reasons need assurance that Shari'ah-compliant cover involves no prohibitions.","topicId":"t16.7","chapter":16,"section":"16.7","topicLabel":"Takaful Challenges","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Assess the status, potential and key challenges facing the global Takaful industry","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.7","pages":[429]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Lack of any Shari'ah scholars willing to work in the industry","Creating awareness that Shari'ah-compliant cover involves no prohibitions, given widespread belief that insurance itself is un-Islamic","Excessive government regulation everywhere","Complete absence of demand for any insurance-like product"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch16-21","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: Family Takaful is generally cash-generating in a company's early years, unlike composite/general Takaful business.","explanation":"False - family Takaful programmes are cash-absorbing in a company's early years, while composite business benefits from expense-and-surplus-driven annual products giving early cash flow.","topicId":"t16.7","chapter":16,"section":"16.7","topicLabel":"Takaful Challenges","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Assess the status, potential and key challenges facing the global Takaful industry","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.7","pages":[429]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch16-22","type":"short","prompt":"What problem does the chapter identify with the re-Takaful facilities currently available in Malaysia, UAE, Bahrain and Saudi Arabia?","explanation":"This connects back to the earlier discussion of Mudarabah model issues (section 16.4.2), showing the same structural problem recurring at the re-Takaful level.","topicId":"t16.7","chapter":16,"section":"16.7","topicLabel":"Takaful Challenges","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Assess the status, potential and key challenges facing the global Takaful industry","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.7","pages":[429,430]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Re-Takaful availability is limited to only a few companies in these countries, and even where available, it is structured mostly on a Mudarabah basis - which is itself Shari'ah-objectionable for the same reasons raised against the Mudarabah model of primary Takaful."},{"id":"q-ch16-23","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which model does the chapter recommend the industry converge on to harmonize Takaful practice, as the basis nearest to Shari'ah principles?","explanation":"The chapter recommends that Shari'ah scholars and practitioners collaborate for convergence on the basis of the Wakalah-Waqf model, which is nearest to the principles of the Shari'ah.","topicId":"t16.7","chapter":16,"section":"16.7","topicLabel":"Takaful Challenges","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Assess the status, potential and key challenges facing the global Takaful industry","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.7","pages":[430]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Pure Wakalah model","Pure Mudarabah model","Wakalah-Waqf model","Conventional co-insurance model"],"correctAnswer":2},{"id":"q-ch16-24","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which 19th-century Islamic jurist was the first to write in detail about modern commercial insurance, and what did he conclude?","explanation":"Ibn Abidin, a widely respected 19th-century jurist, was the first scholar to write about modern commercial insurance (particularly marine insurance of his time) in detail, but did not approve it from the Shari'ah point of view.","topicId":"t16.2.1","chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","topicLabel":"Why Conventional Insurance is Prohibited","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain why conventional insurance is considered non-compliant with Shari'ah","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","pages":[418,419]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Ibn Abidin, who disapproved of it from the Shari'ah point of view","Shaikh Abu Zahra, who fully approved of it","Ibn Taymiyah, who declared it entirely permissible","Nejatullah Siddiqi, who invented the term Takaful"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch16-25","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: 'Ta'mein', the approximate Arabic equivalent of 'insurance', denotes cooperative sharing of losses among a group in the same way Takaful does.","explanation":"False - Ta'mein means to reassure, safeguard and guarantee through indemnity, and denotes fidelity, loyalty, confidence and trust; it refers more to a guarantee than to cooperative sharing of losses among a group, which is why Takaful (not Ta'mein) is the concept that gained wide Shari'ah acceptance.","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"section":"16.3","topicLabel":"The Shari'ah Basis of Takaful","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe the Shari'ah basis of Takaful, including Tabarru' and Waqf","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch16-26","type":"mcq","prompt":"What did Shaikh Abu Zahra conclude about cooperative versus noncooperative insurance?","explanation":"Shaikh Abu Zahra concluded a cooperative and social insurance scheme is, in principle, legitimate, while noncooperative insurance is unacceptable because it contains the traits of gambling, temptation and usury that invalidate the contract.","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"section":"16.3","topicLabel":"The Shari'ah Basis of Takaful","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe the Shari'ah basis of Takaful, including Tabarru' and Waqf","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Both are equally unlawful","A cooperative/social insurance scheme is, in principle, legitimate; noncooperative insurance is unacceptable due to gambling, temptation and usury traits","Both are equally lawful without any Shari'ah distinction","Only life insurance is ever permissible"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch16-27","type":"short","prompt":"What is Muwalat, and how does it relate to the historical Islamic roots of Takaful?","explanation":"This is one of the historical precedents the chapter lists (alongside 'Aqilah and Qasamah) as evidence that Takaful is not a new concept for Islamic commercial law.","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"section":"16.3","topicLabel":"The Shari'ah Basis of Takaful","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe the Shari'ah basis of Takaful, including Tabarru' and Waqf","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Muwalat is a contract in which one party agreed to bequeath his property to another on the understanding that the benefactor would pay any blood money that might eventually be due by the former. Alongside 'Aqilah and Qasamah, it is cited as one of the early Islamic-era practices reflecting the shared-responsibility principle that contemporary jurists see as foundational to Takaful."},{"id":"q-ch16-28","type":"multi","prompt":"Per the chapter's footnote on insurance product categories, which of the following are correctly paired?","explanation":"Life insurance is broadly classified into whole life policies (promising the face value whenever the insured dies) and endowment policies (confined to limited periods) - the reverse of options 3 and 4. General insurance's three main branches are marine, fire and accident (e.g. motor vehicles, aeroplanes).","topicId":"t16.2","chapter":16,"section":"16.2","topicLabel":"The Need for Takaful Cover","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain why conventional insurance is considered non-compliant with Shari'ah","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2","pages":[417]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Life insurance includes whole life policies and endowment policies","General insurance's three main branches are marine, fire and accident","Endowment policies pay the face value whenever the insured dies, with no time limit","Whole life policies are confined to a limited period only"],"correctAnswer":[0,1]},{"id":"q-ch16-29","type":"scenario","prompt":"A Takaful company wants to protect itself against very large claims by transferring some risk to another Takaful operator. On what bases can it structure this re-Takaful arrangement?","explanation":"The chapter states that on the same bases of Tabarru', Waqf and Mudarabah, Takaful companies can arrange re-Takaful, paying an agreed-upon contribution from the Takaful fund to a re-Takaful operator, which in return helps the Takaful company in case of losses.","topicId":"t16.4","chapter":16,"section":"16.4","topicLabel":"How the Takaful System Works","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain how the Takaful system works, including the treatment of underwriting surplus/deficit","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4","pages":[423]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Only via a conventional reinsurance contract with an interest-based reinsurer","On the same bases as primary Takaful - Tabarru', Waqf and Mudarabah - paying an agreed contribution from the Takaful fund to the re-Takaful operator","Re-Takaful is not permitted under any Shari'ah-compliant structure","Only by fully merging with the re-Takaful operator"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch16-30","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which pair of historical Fatawa on insurance, cited in the chapter's appendix, illustrates the century-long scholarly disagreement that Takaful eventually settled?","explanation":"The appendix lists Shaikh Mohammad Abduh's 1900-1901 Fatawa in favor of insurance's validity, contrasted with Shaikh al-Azhar Jad al-Haq Ali Jad al-Haq's 1995 Fatwah against life insurance - opposing views spanning nearly a century that reflect the scholarly discussion eventually converging on Takaful.","topicId":"t16.7","chapter":16,"section":"16.7","topicLabel":"Takaful Challenges","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Assess the status, potential and key challenges facing the global Takaful industry","source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.7","pages":[430,431]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Shaikh Mohammad Abduh's 1900-1901 Fatawa favoring insurance's validity, versus Shaikh al-Azhar's 1995 Fatwah against life insurance","Two identical Fatawa both issued in 1979 in Sudan","A single Fatwah with no recorded opposing view","Fatawa that only ever addressed marine insurance"],"correctAnswer":0}],"flashcards":[{"id":"f16.1","front":"Takaful","back":"Cooperative risk-sharing: participants donate (Tabarru‘) to a fund that compensates members' defined losses; surplus/deficit belongs to participants.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420,421]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.2","front":"Why conventional insurance is prohibited","back":"Riba (direct and indirect), Gharar/Khatar, Qimar and Maisir, and invalid transfer of risk in a commutative contract.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t16.2.1","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","pages":[418,420]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.3","front":"Precaution and Tawakkul","back":"‘Tie your camel first, then put your trust in Allah’ — seeking protection is not against faith.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t16.2","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2","pages":[417,418]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.4","front":"‘Aqilah","back":"Kinsmen's shared responsibility for blood money, approved by the Prophet (pbuh) — foundation of Takaful.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420,421]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.5","front":"Tabarru‘","back":"Donation: participant relinquishes contributions to help fellow participants; removes Gharar.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420,421]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.6","front":"Why the Waqf model is preferred","back":"Donors relinquish ownership yet, unlike Sadaqah, can benefit as Waqf beneficiaries; fits Tabarru‘; favoured by Usmani-led research.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t16.4","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4","pages":[422,423]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.7","front":"Family vs general Takaful contributions","back":"Family: protection (donation) + investment (Mudarabah) [+ fee under Wakalah]. General: entire contribution is a donation.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t16.4","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4","pages":[422,423]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.8","front":"Wakalah vs Mudarabah Takaful models","back":"Wakalah: fixed fee, UWS/UWL to participants. Mudarabah: operator shares profit incl. UWS but bears no UWL — objectionable.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t16.4.1","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","pages":[423,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.9","front":"Box 16.1 Wakalah–Waqf figures","back":"Management fee 25–30%; 70–75% invested; Mudarabah profit 40% company : 60% fund (example).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t16.4.1","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","pages":[423,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.10","front":"Four objections to the Mudarabah model","back":"Donation ≠ capital; operator takes UWS not UWL; non-life capital not returned; Qard/guarantee contradicts Mudarabah.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t16.4.2","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.2","pages":[426,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.11","front":"Takaful vs insurance — surplus","back":"Insurance: UWS belongs to insurer. Takaful: UWS belongs to participants, checking overpricing.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t16.5","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.5","pages":[427,428]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.12","front":"Takaful milestones","back":"1979 Islamic Insurance Co of Sudan; 1984 Malaysia; OIC Fiqh Council approval 1985; growth 10–20% p.a.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t16.6","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.6","pages":[428,429]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.13","front":"Takaful challenges","back":"Awareness (biggest), Waqf adoption, capital, people, re-Takaful, investments, standardisation, regulation, Shari’ah audit.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t16.7","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.7","pages":[429,430]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.14","front":"Why the “no need for cover” belief is a myth","back":"Islam requires avoiding catastrophe where possible and lightening burdens (Qur’an 2:185, 4:28; “Tie your camel”) (p. 418).","category":"Principles","topicId":"t16.2","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2","pages":[417,418]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.15","front":"UWS and UWL","back":"Underwriting surplus: premiums less claims. Underwriting loss: claims in excess of premiums (p. 419).","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t16.2.1","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","pages":[418,420]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.16","front":"Khatar in insurance","back":"Rights and liabilities contingent on an uncertain event; the insurer does not know what it will owe, and premiums are lost if nothing happens (p. 419).","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t16.2.1","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","pages":[418,420]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.17","front":"Ta’mein","back":"The Arabic word for insurance: to reassure and guarantee through indemnity (p. 420).","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420,421]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.18","front":"Qasamah and Mawalat","back":"Qasamah: an oath from the kinsmen of a murdered person. Mawalat: a bequest in return for paying blood money. Both are precedents of shared responsibility (p. 420).","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420,421]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.19","front":"Abu Zahra on insurance","back":"Cooperative and social insurance is legitimate; non-cooperative insurance is invalid (gambling, temptation, usury) (p. 420).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420,421]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.20","front":"Family Takaful and piety","back":"It provides for offspring (“better to leave your heirs wealthy”) and does not insure one’s life (p. 422).","category":"Principles","topicId":"t16.3.1","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3.1","pages":[422,422]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.21","front":"Takaful deficit","back":"If claims exceed the fund and reserves, the operator lends Qard al Hasan to the fund (p. 423).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t16.4","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4","pages":[422,423]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.22","front":"Pure Wakalah model — concern","back":"The fee and sometimes a share of UWS go to the operator, while UWL falls only on participants (the operator gives only Qard); seen as inequitable (p. 424).","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t16.4.1","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","pages":[423,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.23","front":"Waqf model surplus","back":"It belongs to the fund and may be distributed, reserved or reinvested; there is no obligation to distribute (p. 424).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t16.4.1","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","pages":[423,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.24","front":"Takaful pricing principles","back":"Risk premium related to the risk and the same for similar risks; cut fees, not risk premium, for large clients; shareholders bear set-up costs (p. 426).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t16.4.3","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.3","pages":[426,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.25","front":"Order of loss absorption in Takaful","back":"Reserves → Qard from shareholders → general price increase (p. 427).","category":"Risk","topicId":"t16.5","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.5","pages":[427,428]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.26","front":"Insurance density vs penetration","back":"Density: premiums per capita. Penetration: premiums as % of GDP (p. 428).","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t16.6","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.6","pages":[428,429]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f16.27","front":"Re-Takaful problem","back":"Few providers (Malaysia, UAE, Bahrain, Saudi Arabia), mostly on an objectionable Mudarabah basis (pp. 429–430).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t16.7","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.7","pages":[429,430]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-1","front":"The author calls one common reason Muslims avoid insurance a 'myth'. What is it, and how is it countered?","back":"The belief that trusting Allah/destiny makes seeking insurance cover unnecessary. It is countered by the Hadith of the Prophet telling a man to 'tie your camel first, then put your trust in Allah' - taking precautions is not against Islamic belief.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t16.2","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2","pages":[417,418]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-2","front":"In what year did Takaful (Islamic insurance) begin, and how does this compare to Islamic banking's emergence?","back":"Takaful began no earlier than 1979, roughly a decade after Islamic banking emerged in the 1960s-70s.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t16.2","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2","pages":[418]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-3","front":"List the four main Shari'ah objections to conventional insurance.","back":"Riba (interest, direct and indirect), Qimar/Maisir (gambling, gain from chance), Gharar/Khatar (excessive uncertainty about liability/delivery/payment), and invalid transfer of risk from insured to insurer.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t16.2.1","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","pages":[419]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-4","front":"Define Khatar as used in the chapter.","back":"Uncertainty about the transfer of ownership or profitability in a deal where commercial benefit is involved for an uncertain event - found where liability is contingent, delivery is outside a party's control, or payment is uncertain.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t16.2.1","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","pages":[419]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-5","front":"What is 'Aqilah, and why is it significant to Takaful's history?","back":"A historical Arab institution of kinsmen sharing financial responsibility (e.g. for blood money), approved by the Prophet; contemporary jurists see its principle of shared responsibility as the historical foundation of Takaful.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420,421]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-6","front":"What role does Tabarru' play in making Takaful Shari'ah-compliant?","back":"It converts the arrangement from a commutative (exchange) contract vulnerable to Gharar into a donation-based mutual-help arrangement, since a participant relinquishes part of their contribution as Tabarru' to help fellow participants.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[421]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-7","front":"What is the main objective of Takaful from the policyholders' point of view?","back":"Mutual help - not earning profit or windfall gains, unlike conventional insurance; though operators may legitimately earn fees/profit shares as a business.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t16.3.1","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3.1","pages":[422]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-8","front":"How does a family Takaful contribution get split under a Wakalah-basis contract?","back":"Into three parts: a management fee part, a protection part (Tabarru' to the fund), and an investment part (Mudarabah basis, individual rights intact).","category":"Banking","topicId":"t16.4","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4","pages":[422]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-9","front":"Who does the underwriting surplus (UWS) belong to under the settled scholarly consensus on Takaful?","back":"The whole UWS/UWL belongs to the participants/policyholders as a group; the Takaful operator earns only a fee and/or a share of the investment profit, not a share of the underwriting surplus.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t16.4","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4","pages":[423]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-10","front":"What is the difference between Tabarru' treated as Sadaqah versus Tabarru' treated as conditional donation to a Waqf fund?","back":"Like Sadaqah, the donor relinquishes individual ownership; but unlike Sadaqah, under the Waqf model the donor can still benefit as one of the beneficiaries of the fund - making the Waqf model preferable.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t16.4","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4","pages":[423]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-11","front":"Compare the pure Wakalah and pure Mudarabah Takaful models by region and key feature.","back":"Pure Wakalah (mainly Middle East): operator earns a fixed % fee; participants bear the whole UWS/UWL. Pure Mudarabah (mainly Asia-Pacific): profit (investment + UWS) shared per agreed ratio, but operator doesn't bear UWL.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t16.4.1","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","pages":[423,424]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-12","front":"Which Takaful model did research under Shaikh Muhammad Taqi Usmani (40+ scholars) identify as the best practical basis?","back":"A Waqf model, or a combination of Wakalah and Waqf (the Wakalah-Waqf model).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t16.4.1","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","pages":[423]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-13","front":"Why do scholars object to the Mudarabah model of Takaful even though Mudarabah works well for Islamic bank deposits?","back":"A donation (Tabarru') cannot simultaneously be Mudarabah capital; the operator takes a share of UWS without bearing UWL (arguably worse than conventional insurance); non-life premiums aren't returned like Mudarabah capital should be; and requiring Qard al Hasan on a deficit contradicts pure Mudarabah's profit-sharing definition.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t16.4.2","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.2","pages":[426]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-14","front":"How does the Takaful system provide a built-in check against overpricing, unlike conventional insurance?","back":"Losses are absorbed first by reserves, then interest-free shareholder loans, then a general pricing increase - and any surplus always returns to participants in proportion to their contributions, unlike conventional insurance where shareholders retain underwriting profit.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t16.5","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.5","pages":[427]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-15","front":"Under the Islamic model, in whom does insurable interest vest, compared to the conventional model's use of a nominee?","back":"Under the Islamic model, insurable interest vests in the assured or their heirs per inheritance/will principles, rather than a designated nominee as under the conventional system.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t16.5","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.5","pages":[427]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-16","front":"Which was the first Takaful company, and in what year was it established?","back":"The Islamic Insurance Company of Sudan, established in 1979.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t16.6","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.6","pages":[428]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-17","front":"How does Takaful's growth rate compare to the global insurance industry's average growth rate?","back":"Takaful has grown at 10-20% p.a., compared to the global insurance industry's average growth of about 5% p.a.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t16.6","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.6","pages":[428]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-18","front":"What two main reasons does the chapter give for Takaful not matching Islamic banking's global success?","back":"(1) The huge investment required to compete with the established conventional insurance industry; (2) the regulatory changes required (as in Malaysia) to let Takaful compete with conventional insurers on equal terms.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t16.6","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.6","pages":[428]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-19","front":"Why is family Takaful business described as 'cash-absorbing' in a company's early years, unlike composite/general Takaful?","back":"Composite business offers annual, expense-and-surplus-driven products giving early cash flow, while family Takaful programmes require heavier upfront investment before returns materialize - solvable only by enhancing the company's capital base.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t16.7","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.7","pages":[429]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-20","front":"What problem exists with the re-Takaful (reinsurance) facilities that are available in Malaysia, UAE, Bahrain and Saudi Arabia?","back":"They are mostly structured on a Mudarabah basis, which is itself Shari'ah-objectionable for the same reasons that apply to the Mudarabah model of primary Takaful.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t16.7","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.7","pages":[429,430]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-21","front":"What does 'Ta'mein' mean, and why did scholars ultimately favor 'Takaful' instead?","back":"Ta'mein means to reassure, safeguard and guarantee through indemnity - closer to a guarantee than cooperative loss-sharing. Scholars instead favored Takaful because it converts the contract into a contributory, mutual-help arrangement rather than a guarantee-style indemnity.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-22","front":"Who was the first Islamic jurist to write in detail about modern commercial insurance, and what was his conclusion?","back":"Ibn Abidin, a widely respected 19th-century jurist, wrote about modern commercial (particularly marine) insurance in detail but did not approve it from the Shari'ah point of view.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t16.2.1","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","pages":[418,419]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-23","front":"When and how did marine insurance, the first form of commercial insurance, develop?","back":"It probably began at the end of the 12th century and took a formal system in the 17th century, when the marine business developed on a massive scale.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t16.2.1","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","pages":[418,419]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-24","front":"What did Shaikh Abu Zahra (20th-century jurist) conclude about cooperative versus noncooperative insurance?","back":"A cooperative/social insurance scheme is, in principle, legitimate; noncooperative insurance is unacceptable because it contains the traits of gambling, temptation and usury that invalidate the contract.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-25","front":"What is Muwalat, and how does it relate to Takaful's historical roots?","back":"A contract in which one party agreed to bequeath property to another on the understanding that the benefactor would pay any blood money eventually due by the former - cited, alongside 'Aqilah and Qasamah, as an early Islamic precedent for shared-risk/mutual-responsibility arrangements.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t16.3","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.3","pages":[420]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-26","front":"How does the book classify insurance products by type?","back":"Two main categories: life insurance (whole life policies, paying face value whenever the insured dies, versus endowment policies, confined to limited periods) and general insurance (marine, fire, and accident branches, e.g. motor vehicles, aeroplanes).","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t16.2","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2","pages":[417]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-27","front":"On what bases can a Takaful company arrange re-Takaful (reinsurance for Takaful operators)?","back":"On the same bases as primary Takaful - Tabarru', Waqf and Mudarabah - paying an agreed contribution from the Takaful fund to a re-Takaful operator, who helps the Takaful company in case of losses.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t16.4","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4","pages":[423]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch16-28","front":"Contrast two opposing historical Fatawa on insurance cited in the chapter's appendix.","back":"Shaikh Mohammad Abduh (ex-Grand Mufti of Egypt) issued two Fatawa in 1900-1901 favoring the validity of insurance; nearly a century later, Shaikh al-Azhar Jad al-Haq Ali Jad al-Haq issued a 1995 Fatwah against life insurance specifically - illustrating the long, unresolved scholarly debate that Takaful eventually settled.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t16.7","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.7","pages":[430,431]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}],"exam":[{"id":"e16.1","kind":"long","prompt":"Critically evaluate the models of Takaful and explain why the Wakalah–Waqf model is regarded as closest to Shari’ah.","expectedStructure":["Principles: Tabarru‘, mutual help, operator not risk-taker","Wakalah model and issues","Mudarabah model and four objections","Wakalah–Mudarabah issues","Waqf and Wakalah–Waqf (Box 16.1)","Usmani-led research and convergence"],"keyConcepts":["UWS","UWL","Qard al Hasan","Waqf"],"essentialPoints":["Surplus to participants"],"commonMistakes":["Letting operator share surplus as risk-taker"],"topicId":"t16.4.1","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.1","pages":[423,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e16.2","kind":"short","prompt":"Why is conventional insurance considered unlawful and how does Takaful address each objection?","expectedStructure":["Riba — compliant investment and no exchange of premium for sum","Gharar — Tabarru‘ removes commutative exchange","Maisir/Qimar — no gain at others' loss","Risk transfer — mutual sharing"],"keyConcepts":["Khatar","commutative contract"],"essentialPoints":["Ibn Abdin"],"commonMistakes":["Saying seeking protection is prohibited"],"topicId":"t16.2.1","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","pages":[418,420]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e16.3","kind":"conceptual","prompt":"Compare Takaful with conventional insurance.","expectedStructure":["Basis","Risk","Surplus","Pricing","Life policies","Investments"],"keyConcepts":["mutual help","adequacy"],"essentialPoints":["Use a table"],"commonMistakes":["Calling Takaful risk-free"],"topicId":"t16.5","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.5","pages":[427,428]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e16.4","kind":"viva","prompt":"What are the main challenges facing the Takaful industry?","expectedStructure":["Awareness","Capital","People","Re-Takaful","Standardisation","Regulation"],"keyConcepts":["family Takaful"],"essentialPoints":["Awareness is the biggest"],"commonMistakes":["Ignoring re-Takaful"],"topicId":"t16.7","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.7","pages":[429,430]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e16.5","kind":"scenario","prompt":"A newly formed Takaful company asks which model to adopt and how to price large corporate policies. Advise it.","expectedStructure":["Compare models","Recommend Wakalah–Waqf","Surplus and deficit","Pricing","Governance"],"keyConcepts":["Waqf","Tabarru‘","UWS","Qard al Hasan"],"essentialPoints":["Wakalah inequitable; Mudarabah objections (pp. 424, 426)","Wakalah–Waqf recommended by 40+ scholars (p. 423)","UWS/UWL for participants; Qard for deficits (pp. 423–424)","Reduce the fee, not the risk premium, for large clients (p. 426)","Shari’ah board and audit (p. 429)"],"commonMistakes":["Giving the operator a UWS share","Charging management expenses to the fund under Mudarabah"],"topicId":"t16.4.3","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.4.3","pages":[426,426]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-15","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Explain why conventional insurance is considered non-compliant with the Shari'ah, and how Takaful addresses these concerns.","expectedStructure":["State the prohibitions implicated (Riba, Gharar, Maisir)","Explain the risk-transfer vs risk-sharing distinction","Describe the Tabarru'/Waqf-fund structure of Takaful"],"keyConcepts":["Takaful","Tabarru'","Riba, Gharar, Maisir in insurance"],"essentialPoints":["Conventional insurance is questioned mainly because of the Riba, Gharar and gambling elements involved in exchanging a premium for an uncertain future benefit.","In Takaful, participants relinquish ownership of their contribution as a donation (Tabarru') into a common fund (often a Waqf), which pays losses to members and shares any surplus among them.","Takaful funds are invested only in Shari'ah-compliant avenues, and the operator manages the business, sharing investment profit with participants rather than retaining risk-transfer premiums as company profit."],"commonMistakes":["Claiming Islam prohibits seeking any protection against loss — the book explicitly states this belief ('trust in Allah alone') is a myth; the actual objection is to the Riba/Gharar/Maisir structure of the conventional contract.","Describing Takaful as simply 'insurance run by Muslims' rather than explaining the structural donation/mutual-sharing basis."],"topicId":"t16.2.1","chapter":16,"source":{"chapter":16,"section":"16.2.1","pages":[418]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}]});
